    "slug": "leftist-heaps",
    "markdown": "# Leftist Heaps: Merge-Oriented Trees & Null Path Length Bias\n\nStandard binary heaps require array indices to be contiguous, making the merging of two heaps an $O(N)$ copying process. To merge priority queues in logarithmic time ($O(\\log N)$), we use a pointer-based binary tree called a **Leftist Heap**.\n\nThis chapter explores **Leftist Heaps**. We focus on Null Path Length (NPL) calculations, structural bias properties, and recursive merging paths.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Null Path Length (NPL) & The Leftist Property\n\nThe **Null Path Length (NPL)** of a node $X$, denoted by $\\text{npl}(X)$, is the length of the shortest path from $X$ to a node without two children (a leaf or half-empty node).\n- If $X$ is `null`, $\\text{npl}(X) = -1$.\n- For any active node $X$:\n\n$$\\text{npl}(X) = 1 + \\min(\\text{npl}(\\text{left}(X)), \\text{npl}(\\text{right}(X)))$$\n\nA binary tree is a **Leftist Tree** if it satisfies the leftist property: for every node $X$:\n\n$$\\text{npl}(\\text{left}(X)) \\ge \\text{npl}(\\text{right}(X))$$\n\nThis property forces the right path of the tree to be short, containing at most $O(\\log N)$ nodes.\n\n### Leftist Heap NPL Mapping\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#a855f7\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Leftist Heap Node NPL Counts</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 60)\">\n    {/* Root */}\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"20\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#a855f7\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"23\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">10 (npl=1)</text>\n\n    {/* Left Child */}\n    <path d=\"M 90,30 L 60,60\" stroke=\"#a855f7\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"70\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"73\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">15 (npl=1)</text>\n    \n    <path d=\"M 40,80 L 25,110\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"120\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    <text x=\"20\" y=\"123\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">20 (npl=0)</text>\n\n    {/* Right Child */}\n    <path d=\"M 110,30 L 140,60\" stroke=\"#a855f7\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"70\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"73\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">35 (npl=0)</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Core Algorithm: Logarithmic Merge\n\nAll leftist heap operations (insert, extract-min) are mapped directly to a single helper function: `merge(H1, H2)`.\n\nTo merge two heaps recursively:\n1. Ensure the root of `H1` has a smaller key than `H2` (swap if necessary).\n2. Recursively merge the right sub-heap of `H1` with `H2`:\n\n$$\\text{right}(H1) = \\text{merge}(\\text{right}(H1), H2)$$\n\n3. If the leftist property is violated ($\\text{npl}(\\text{left}(H1)) < \\text{npl}(\\text{right}(H1))$), swap the left and right children of `H1`.\n4. Update the NPL of `H1`:\n\n$$\\text{npl}(H1) = 1 + \\text{npl}(\\text{right}(H1))$$\n\nBecause we always traverse down the right path (which is at most $O(\\log N)$ elements), the entire merge completes in $O(\\log N)$ steps.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Design Linked List (LeetCode 707)](https://leetcode.com/problems/design-linked-list/): Pointer tree layouts.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Build pointer manipulation skills.\n* [Merge Two Sorted Lists (LeetCode 21)](https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-two-sorted-lists/): Recursive node mapping.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Teaches you recursive merging structures.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [Merge k Sorted Lists (LeetCode 23)](https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-k-sorted-lists/): Priority queues.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Optimizes sorting queues by merging components.\n",
