    "slug": "bfs-traversal",
    "markdown": "# BFS Traversal: Radial Wavefront Expansion & Shortest Paths\n\nTo locate shortest paths in an unweighted graph, examining deep paths first wastes time exploring wrong directions. Instead, we scan outward level by level using **Breadth-First Search (BFS)**.\n\nThis chapter explores **BFS Traversal**. We focus on queue-based wavefront expansions, visited set state machines, and short-path search properties.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Radial Wavefronts: The Queue Loop Invariant\n\nBFS explores a graph layer by layer, starting from a source node. To track the frontier of exploration:\n1. Initialize a queue and a visited set. Add the source node to both.\n2. While the queue is not empty:\n   - Dequeue the front node `curr`.\n   - Process `curr`.\n   - For each unvisited neighbor of `curr`, mark it as visited and enqueue it.\n\n### BFS Wavefront Expansion\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">BFS Wavefront Levels</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 60)\">\n    {/* Level 0 */}\n    <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"53\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Start</text>\n\n    {/* Level 1 */}\n    <path d=\"M 65,50 L 135,25\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"20\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"23\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">L1 (Node A)</text>\n\n    <path d=\"M 65,50 L 135,75\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"80\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"83\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">L1 (Node B)</text>\n\n    {/* Wavefront Arc */}\n    <path d=\"M 120,5 Q 160,50 120,95\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 1\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-bfs-wave)\"/>\n    <text x=\"175\" y=\"53\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"monospace\">Wavefront</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-bfs-wave\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Binary Tree Level Order Traversal (LeetCode 102)](https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-level-order-traversal/): Level-by-level queue loops.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Build queue traversal foundations.\n* [Flood Fill (LeetCode 733)](https://leetcode.com/problems/flood-fill/): Image pixel expansions.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Connects level search concepts to 2D grid partitions.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [Rotting Oranges (LeetCode 994)](https://leetcode.com/problems/rotting-oranges/): Multi-source BFS wavefronts.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Teaches you to coordinate multiple search points concurrently.\n* [Word Ladder (LeetCode 127)](https://leetcode.com/problems/word-ladder/): String graph edges.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Uses BFS to solve shortest transformation path limits.\n",
