    "slug": "game-math-perspective",
    "markdown": "# Game Mathematics: Perspective & Depth Mechanics\n\nPerspective projection models the way the human eye and camera lenses perceive depth, scaling down the size of 3D objects inversely proportional to their distance from the camera coordinate center.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Perspective Formula\n\nA standard perspective projection is a non-linear operation because it involves dividing coordinates by their depth ($z$):\n\n$$x' = \\frac{x \\cdot \\text{focus}}{-z}, \\quad y' = \\frac{y \\cdot \\text{focus}}{-z}$$\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Focal Point (Eye) -->\n  <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"35\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\">Eye</text>\n\n  <!-- Projection Screen (Near Plane) -->\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"30\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\">Screen (Near Plane)</text>\n\n  <!-- Similar Triangles rays -->\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n\n  <!-- Projected Object (Height h') -->\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"4\" />\n  <text x=\"160\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\">h'</text>\n\n  <!-- Physical Object (Height H) -->\n  <line x1=\"450\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"3\" />\n  <text x=\"460\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\">H</text>\n  \n  <!-- Depth Z dimension markers -->\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"190\" stroke=\"#a1a1aa\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"210\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\">Distance (-z)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nUsing similar triangles, we find that the projected height $h'$ of a 3D object of height $H$ at distance $-z$ is:\n$$h' = \\frac{H \\cdot \\text{focus}}{-z}$$\n\nBecause we divide by $-z$ (where looking direction is along the negative Z-axis), the projected height decreases as depth increases.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Setting Up the Perspective Divide\n\nBecause dividing by coordinate variables is a non-linear step, we cannot perform it directly using a standard linear matrix multiplication. Instead, the projection matrix maps the coordinate's depth to the vector's $w$ component:\n\n$$\\mathbf{v}_{\\text{clip}} = \\begin{bmatrix} x_{\\text{clip}} \\\\ y_{\\text{clip}} \\\\ z_{\\text{clip}} \\\\ w_{\\text{clip}} \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} x \\cdot f_x \\\\ y \\cdot f_y \\\\ A \\cdot z + B \\\\ -z \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nFollowing matrix multiplication, the GPU hardware performs the **perspective divide** step, dividing the coordinates by the $w$ component to yield NDC coordinates:\n\n$$\\mathbf{v}_{\\text{ndc}} = \\begin{bmatrix} x_{\\text{clip}}/w_{\\text{clip}} \\\\ y_{\\text{clip}}/w_{\\text{clip}} \\\\ z_{\\text{clip}}/w_{\\text{clip}} \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} -x \\cdot f_x / z \\\\ -y \\cdot f_y / z \\\\ -(A \\cdot z + B) / z \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\n---\n\n## 3. Depth Buffer ($Z$) Calculation\n\nTo determine which surfaces render on top of others, game engines write coordinate depth values into the **Z-Buffer** (or depth buffer). Like the perspective divide, the depth value stored in the buffer is non-linear:\n\n$$z_{\\text{depth}} = \\frac{A \\cdot z + B}{-z} = -A - \\frac{B}{z}$$\n\nWhere $A$ and $B$ are constants derived from the Near ($n$) and Far ($f$) planes:\n\n$$A = -\\frac{f+n}{f-n}, \\quad B = -\\frac{2fn}{f-n}$$\n\n#### The Precision Distribution Problem:\nBecause this mapping is proportional to $1/z$, depth buffer precision is distributed non-linearly. The majority of the precision is concentrated near the Near plane:\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 130\" width=\"100%\" height=\"130\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Line bar -->\n  <rect x=\"50\" y=\"60\" width=\"400\" height=\"8\" fill=\"#27272a\" rx=\"4\" />\n  \n  <!-- Left Side: high precision dense block (Near) -->\n  <rect x=\"50\" y=\"60\" width=\"80\" height=\"8\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" rx=\"4\" />\n  <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"64\" r=\"5.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"35\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\">Near (0.1m)</text>\n  \n  <text x=\"90\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\">90% of Z-Precision</text>\n  \n  <!-- Right Side: low precision sparse block (Far) -->\n  <circle cx=\"450\" cy=\"64\" r=\"5.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"420\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\">Far (100m)</text>\n  \n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\">10% of Precision</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nFor instance, if $n=0.1$ and $f=100$, up to $90\\%$ of the depth buffer's numeric values are spent representing the first few meters from the camera, leaving very little precision for objects in the far distance.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Z-Fighting and the Reverse-Z Solution\n\nWhen two flat surfaces (such as a poster on a wall) are positioned far away from the camera, the precision loss in the depth buffer can cause their calculated depth values to merge. The GPU cannot distinguish which surface is closer, leading to a flickering artifact known as **Z-Fighting**.\n\n```\nFlickering pixels (Z-Fighting):\n ▒█▒█▒█▒█▒ (Wall and poster depth values overlap)\n```\n\n### The Reverse-Z Solution\nTo resolve this precision drop, modern game engines use the **Reverse-Z** technique:\n1.  Map the Near plane to depth $1.0$ and the Far plane to depth $0.0$.\n2.  Pair this with a floating-point depth buffer format (such as `D32_FLOAT`).\n\nBecause floating-point formats provide exponentially higher precision for numbers close to $0.0$, placing the Far plane at $0.0$ balances the logarithmic distribution of depth values, creating a nearly uniform distribution of precision across the entire frustum volume and eliminating Z-fighting.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implementation Example (Python)\n\n```python\ndef get_depth_buffer_value(z, near, far):\n    \"\"\"\n    Simulates the standard non-linear value written to a Z-buffer.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Vulkan/DirectX coordinate conventions\n    return (far / (far - near)) * (1.0 - (near / z))\n\n# Evaluation of precision drops:\nnear = 0.1\nfar = 100.0\n\n# Close proximity values:\nz_close1 = 0.2\nz_close2 = 0.3\ndelta_close = get_depth_buffer_value(z_close2, near, far) - get_depth_buffer_value(z_close1, near, far)\n\n# Far proximity values:\nz_far1 = 80.0\nz_far2 = 80.1\ndelta_far = get_depth_buffer_value(z_far2, near, far) - get_depth_buffer_value(z_far1, near, far)\n\nprint(f\"Close range precision window (0.2m -> 0.3m): {delta_close:.6f}\")\nprint(f\"Far range precision window (80.0m -> 80.1m):  {delta_far:.8f}\")\n```\n",
    "title": "Perspective",
    "tagline": "Perspective division, Z-buffer depth non-linearity, and Reverse-Z precision mapping.",
    "category": "Game Developer",
