    "slug": "game-phys-restitution",
    "markdown": "# Game Physics: Coefficient of Restitution\n\nWhen two rigid bodies collide, they deform and recover, transferring kinetic energy between them. The **Coefficient of Restitution ($e$)** represents the ratio of relative speeds before and after a collision, defining how bouncy the collision is.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Defining the Coefficient ($e$)\n\nFor a 1D collision along a collision normal, the coefficient $e$ is defined as:\n\n$$e = -\\frac{v_{2,f} - v_{1,f}}{v_{2,i} - v_{1,i}}$$\n\nWhere:\n*   $v_{1,i}, v_{2,i}$ are the initial velocities before impact.\n*   $v_{1,f}, v_{2,f}$ are the final velocities after impact.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 180\" width=\"100%\" height=\"180\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Collision Normal axis -->\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  \n  <!-- Left Object -->\n  <circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"90\" r=\"30\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"90\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <line x1=\"160\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-blue)\" />\n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">v1</text>\n  \n  <!-- Right Object -->\n  <circle cx=\"340\" cy=\"90\" r=\"30\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <circle cx=\"340\" cy=\"90\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <line x1=\"340\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-amber)\" />\n  <text x=\"295\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">v2</text>\n  \n  <!-- Collision point normal guide -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"215\" y=\"30\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Collision Plane</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n### Values of $e$:\n*   **$e = 1.0$ (Perfect Elastic Collision):** No kinetic energy is lost. The objects bounce off each other with the same relative speed (e.g. billiard balls).\n*   **$e = 0.0$ (Perfect Inelastic Collision):** Maximum kinetic energy is lost. The objects stick together and move with a shared velocity (e.g. clay hitting a floor).\n*   **$0.0 < e < 1.0$ (Real-world / Plastic Collision):** Some energy is lost to heat and deformation (most game objects use values between $0.2$ and $0.8$).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Rotational Collision Impulse Equation\n\nTo resolve a collision, physics engines calculate a single scalar impulse $j$ along the contact normal $\\mathbf{n}$ and apply it to both bodies. \n\nFor rigid bodies that can both translate and rotate, the **Rotational Collision Impulse** formula is:\n\n$$j = \\frac{-(1 + e) (\\mathbf{v}_{\\text{rel}} \\cdot \\mathbf{n})}{\\frac{1}{m_1} + \\frac{1}{m_2} + \\frac{(\\mathbf{r}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n})^2}{I_1} + \\frac{(\\mathbf{r}_2 \\times \\mathbf{n})^2}{I_2}}$$\n\nWhere:\n*   $\\mathbf{v}_{\\text{rel}} = (\\mathbf{v}_2 + \\mathbf{\\omega}_2 \\times \\mathbf{r}_2) - (\\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{\\omega}_1 \\times \\mathbf{r}_1)$ is the relative velocity at the contact point.\n*   $\\mathbf{r}_1, \\mathbf{r}_2$ are vector offsets pointing from the centers of mass of the objects to the contact point.\n*   $m_1, m_2$ and $I_1, I_2$ are the masses and moments of inertia of the objects.\n\nOnce $j$ is calculated, the linear and angular velocities are updated:\n\n$$\\mathbf{v}_1' = \\mathbf{v}_1 - \\frac{j \\mathbf{n}}{m_1}, \\quad \\mathbf{\\omega}_1' = \\mathbf{\\omega}_1 - I_1^{-1} (\\mathbf{r}_1 \\times j\\mathbf{n})$$\n$$\\mathbf{v}_2' = \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\frac{j \\mathbf{n}}{m_2}, \\quad \\mathbf{\\omega}_2' = \\mathbf{\\omega}_2 + I_2^{-1} (\\mathbf{r}_2 \\times j\\mathbf{n})$$\n\n---\n\n## 3. Implementation Example (Python)\n\n```python\nclass PhysicsSphere:\n    def __init__(self, mass, velocity):\n        self.mass = float(mass)\n        self.velocity = float(velocity) # 1D velocity\n\ndef resolve_1d_collision(sphere_a, sphere_b, e):\n    # Relative velocity before collision\n    rel_vel = sphere_b.velocity - sphere_a.velocity\n    \n    # Only resolve if objects are moving towards each other\n    if rel_vel > 0:\n        return\n        \n    # Calculate scalar impulse j: j = -(1+e)*rel_vel / (1/ma + 1/mb)\n    inv_mass_sum = (1.0 / sphere_a.mass) + (1.0 / sphere_b.mass)\n    j = -(1.0 + e) * rel_vel / inv_mass_sum\n    \n    # Update velocities\n    sphere_a.velocity -= j / sphere_a.mass\n    sphere_b.velocity += j / sphere_b.mass\n\n# Test: 2kg sphere moving at 4m/s hits 1kg stationary sphere with e=0.5\nsa = PhysicsSphere(2.0, 4.0)\nsb = PhysicsSphere(1.0, 0.0)\nresolve_1d_collision(sa, sb, 0.5)\n\nprint(\"sa velocity:\", sa.velocity) # Expected: 2.0 m/s\nprint(\"sb velocity:\", sb.velocity) # Expected: 4.0 m/s\n```\n",
    "title": "Coefficient of Restitution",
    "tagline": "Elastic vs. inelastic impacts, rotational collision impulse, and velocity updates.",
    "category": "Game Developer",
    "readTime": 15,
    "lastUpdated": "July 2026",
