    "slug": "game-phys-angular-vel",
    "markdown": "# Game Physics: Angular Velocity\n\n**Angular Velocity ($\\mathbf{\\omega}$)** represents the rate at which an object rotates, as well as the axis of that rotation. While linear velocity describes an object's displacement through space, angular velocity describes the rate of change of its orientation.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Vector Representation\n\nIn 3D space, angular velocity is represented as a vector $\\mathbf{\\omega} = (\\omega_x, \\omega_y, \\omega_z)$:\n*   **Direction:** The direction of the vector defines the **axis of rotation** (according to the right-hand rule: curl your fingers in the direction of rotation, and your thumb points in the direction of the vector).\n*   **Magnitude:** The length of the vector $\\|\\mathbf{\\omega}\\|$ defines the **rotation speed** in radians per second.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Velocity of a Point on a Rotating Body\n\nFor any point on a rigid body located at an offset vector $\\mathbf{r}$ relative to the rotation origin, its instantaneous linear velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ generated by the rotation is calculated using the vector cross product:\n\n$$\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{\\omega} \\times \\mathbf{r}$$\n\nBecause of the cross product, the resulting velocity vector is always perpendicular to both the rotation axis ($\\mathbf{\\omega}$) and the offset vector ($\\mathbf{r}$), pointing along the tangent of the rotation path.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Rotating Disk base -->\n  <ellipse cx=\"250\" cy=\"130\" rx=\"140\" ry=\"50\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  \n  <!-- Axis of rotation vector (omega - pointing UP) -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rose)\" />\n  <text x=\"260\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">ω (Rotation Axis/Speed)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Point on disk -->\n  <circle cx=\"340\" cy=\"115\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  \n  <!-- Radius vector r -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"335\" y2=\"116\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-blue)\" />\n  <text x=\"290\" y=\"110\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">r (Offset)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Instantaneous velocity vector v = omega x r (pointing tangentially forward) -->\n  <line x1=\"340\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-emerald)\" />\n  <text x=\"375\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"13\">v = ω × r</text>\n  \n  <!-- Rotation arrow guide on disk edge -->\n  <path d=\"M 370 145 C 385 135 390 120 385 110\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#a1a1aa\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-zinc)\" />\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Integrating Orientation with Angular Velocity\n\nUpdating an object's rotation over time requires updating its orientation quaternion $\\mathbf{q}$. The rate of change of a quaternion is defined by:\n\n$$\\dot{\\mathbf{q}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\mathbf{\\omega}_q \\mathbf{q}$$\n\nWhere $\\mathbf{\\omega}_q = [0, \\omega_x, \\omega_y, \\omega_z]$ is the angular velocity represented as a pure quaternion with a real part of $0$.\n\nTo update the orientation in each frame using Euler integration:\n\n$$\\mathbf{q}(t + \\Delta t) = \\text{Normalize}\\left( \\mathbf{q}(t) + \\frac{1}{2} \\Delta t \\mathbf{\\omega}_q \\mathbf{q}(t) \\right)$$\n\nWe must re-normalize the quaternion in every frame to prevent floating-point drift from distorting the scaling factor.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python)\n\n```python\nclass Quaternion:\n    def __init__(self, w, x, y, z):\n        self.w = float(w)\n        self.x = float(x)\n        self.y = float(y)\n        self.z = float(z)\n        \n    def normalize(self):\n        mag = math.sqrt(self.w**2 + self.x**2 + self.y**2 + self.z**2)\n        if mag > 0.0:\n            self.w /= mag\n            self.x /= mag\n            self.y /= mag\n            self.z /= mag\n            \n    def update_angular_velocity(self, omega, dt):\n        # omega: tuple/list (wx, wy, wz)\n        # dq/dt = 0.5 * omega * q\n        half_dt = 0.5 * dt\n        \n        # Calculate quaternion multiplication output (omega * self)\n        dw = -self.x * omega[0] - self.y * omega[1] - self.z * omega[2]\n        dx =  self.w * omega[0] + self.y * omega[2] - self.z * omega[1]\n        dy =  self.w * omega[1] - self.x * omega[2] + self.z * omega[0]\n        dz =  self.w * omega[2] + self.x * omega[1] - self.y * omega[0]\n        \n        # Integrate orientation\n        self.w += dw * half_dt\n        self.x += dx * half_dt\n        self.y += dy * half_dt\n        self.z += dz * half_dt\n        \n        self.normalize()\n```\n",
    "title": "Angular Velocity",
    "tagline": "Spin rate vectors, cross product velocities, and quaternion integration.",
    "category": "Game Developer",
    "readTime": 15,
