    "slug": "game-phys-friction",
    "markdown": "# Game Physics: Friction Models\n\n**Friction** is the tangential force that opposes relative lateral motion (sliding) between two surfaces in contact. Implementing friction is critical for stopping objects from sliding endlessly down slopes or allowing wheels to roll.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Coulomb Friction Model\n\nIn game physics, we approximate surface friction using the classical **Coulomb Friction Model**. The model states that the maximum friction force ($F_f$) is proportional to the normal force ($F_n$) pushing the surfaces together:\n\n$$F_f \\le \\mu F_n$$\n\nWhere $\\mu$ is the **Coefficient of Friction**, which depends on the materials in contact.\n\nWe divide friction into two regimes:\n\n### 1.1 Static Friction\nActs when the relative tangential velocity between surfaces is zero ($v_t = 0$). It prevents objects from starting to slide:\n\n$$F_f < \\mu_s F_n$$\n\nIf the applied lateral force exceeds this threshold, the object breaks free and begins to slide.\n\n### 1.2 Kinetic (Dynamic) Friction\nActs when the surfaces are sliding relative to each other ($v_t \\neq 0$). It operates in the direction opposite to the sliding velocity, slowing it down:\n\n$$F_f = \\mu_k F_n$$\n\nTypically, $\\mu_k \\le \\mu_s$ (it is easier to keep an object sliding than it is to start sliding it from a standstill).\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Friction Cone\n\nIn 3D space, the normal force acts along the contact normal $\\mathbf{n}$, while the friction force acts on the 2D tangent plane perpendicular to $\\mathbf{n}$. \n\nThe boundary separating **sticking** (static friction) from **slipping** (kinetic friction) forms a cone in 3D force space called the **Friction Cone**:\n\n$$\\|\\mathbf{F}_t\\| \\le \\mu F_n$$\n\nWhere $\\mathbf{F}_t = (F_{t1}, F_{t2})$ is the 2D tangential friction force vector.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Contact Plane -->\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  \n  <!-- Contact Point -->\n  <circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"180\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  \n  <!-- Normal Vector n -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-blue)\" />\n  <text x=\"260\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">n (Contact Normal)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Friction Cone outline -->\n  <!-- slope is defined by mu (e.g. mu = 0.5) -->\n  <polygon points=\"250,180 190,60 310,60\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n  \n  <!-- Force vector inside cone (Sticking) -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-emerald)\" />\n  <text x=\"160\" y=\"110\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">F_net (Sticking)</text>\n  \n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"110\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\">Friction Cone</text>\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"125\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">||Ft|| &lt;= μ * Fn</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n*   **Inside Cone:** The combined contact force lies inside the cone. The friction is static; the object does not slip.\n*   **On Cone Surface:** The contact force reaches the cone boundary. The object slips, and friction is limited to the boundary value $\\mu F_n$.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Impulse-Based Friction Resolution\n\nIn impulse-based velocity solvers (like those in Unity or Unreal), friction is resolved by applying a tangential impulse $P_t$ at the contact point.\n\n1.  Calculate the normal impulse $P_n$ needed to prevent penetration.\n2.  Calculate the raw tangential impulse $P_{t,\\text{raw}}$ needed to eliminate relative tangential velocity.\n3.  Clamp the tangential impulse using the friction coefficient limit:\n    $$P_t = \\text{Clamp}(P_{t,\\text{raw}}, -\\mu P_n, \\mu P_n)$$\n4.  Apply $P_t$ to the bodies.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python)\n\n```python\nclass ContactPoint:\n    def __init__(self, normal_impulse):\n        self.normal_impulse = float(normal_impulse)\n        \n    def solve_friction(self, relative_tangential_velocity, mass_effective, mu):\n        # 1. Tangential impulse needed to stop sliding: Pt = -v_t * m_eff\n        raw_tangent_impulse = -relative_tangential_velocity * mass_effective\n        \n        # 2. Coulomb limit: max_impulse = mu * Pn\n        max_friction_impulse = mu * self.normal_impulse\n        \n        # 3. Clamp tangential impulse to friction cone\n        clamped_tangent_impulse = max(min(raw_tangent_impulse, max_friction_impulse), -max_friction_impulse)\n        \n        # Returns the resolved friction impulse and whether slipping occurred\n        slipping = abs(clamped_tangent_impulse - raw_tangent_impulse) > 1e-5\n        return clamped_tangent_impulse, slipping\n\n# Test: Normal impulse = 10N, relative tangential velocity = 5m/s, mu = 0.3\ncontact = ContactPoint(10.0)\nimpulse, slipped = contact.solve_friction(5.0, 1.0, 0.3)\nprint(\"Friction Impulse applied:\", impulse) # Expected: -3.0 (Clamped from -5.0)\nprint(\"Slipping occurred:\", slipped)       # Expected: True\n```\n",
    "title": "Friction Models",
    "tagline": "Coulomb friction, static vs. kinetic regimes, friction cones, and impulse clamping.",
    "category": "Game Developer",
    "readTime": 15,
    "lastUpdated": "July 2026",
