    "slug": "game-phys-narrow-phase",
    "markdown": "# Game Physics: Narrow Phase Collision Detection\n\nCollision detection is split into two phases:\n1.  **Broad Phase:** Filters out objects that are far apart using fast approximations (e.g. bounding spheres, AABBs, spatial hashing).\n2.  **Narrow Phase:** Performs precise geometric tests on the remaining potential collision pairs to determine if they intersect, generating the mathematical data needed to resolve the collision.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Contact Manifold\n\nIf the narrow phase detects a collision, it does not just return `true`. Instead, it generates a data structure called the **Contact Manifold**. This structure contains:\n\n*   **Contact Normal ($\\mathbf{n}$):** The unit direction vector pointing from Body A to Body B, along which the collision force should be applied to push them apart.\n*   **Penetration Depth ($d$):** The distance of overlap between the two shapes.\n*   **Contact Points ($\\mathbf{p}_i$):** The exact coordinate points on the surfaces of the shapes where contact occurs.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Object A (Rotated Box) -->\n  <polygon points=\"120,60 260,110 220,170 80,120\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Body A</text>\n  \n  <!-- Object B (Sphere) -->\n  <circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"140\" r=\"50\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"290\" y=\"145\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Body B</text>\n  \n  <!-- Intersection Zone Highlight (Shared points) -->\n  <path d=\"M 230 140 C 235 120 250 110 260 110 L 220 170 Z\" fill=\"#ef4444\" fill-opacity=\"0.2\" />\n  \n  <!-- Contact Points -->\n  <circle cx=\"242\" cy=\"125\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">p1 (Contact Pt)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Contact Normal and Penetration depth -->\n  <!-- Normal vector pointing from A to B -->\n  <line x1=\"242\" y1=\"125\" x2=\"285\" y2=\"105\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-emerald)\" />\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">n (Normal)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Penetration depth indicator -->\n  <line x1=\"242\" y1=\"125\" x2=\"255\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Penetration Depth (d)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Narrow Phase Algorithms\n\nDepending on the shapes involved, the narrow phase runs specialized geometric algorithms:\n\n*   **Sphere-Sphere Collision:** Simple distance check. If the distance between centers is less than the sum of their radii, they collide:\n    $$d = (r_A + r_B) - \\|\\mathbf{c}_B - \\mathbf{c}_A\\| > 0$$\n*   **Separating Axis Theorem (SAT):** Projected overlap checks. If a shadow projection axis exists where the shapes do not overlap, they do not collide. Very fast for boxes and polygons.\n*   **GJK & EPA (Gilbert-Johnson-Keerthi & Expansion Polytope):** Uses Minkowski differences and simplex geometries to check collision and calculate penetration for general convex shapes.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Resolving the Contact Manifold\n\nOnce the contact manifold is generated, the physics engine feeds this data to the velocity solver:\n\n1.  **Velocity Solver:** Applies impulses along the normal ($\\mathbf{n}$) to stop the relative velocity and prevent further penetration.\n2.  **Position Solver (Pseudo-Impulse / Baumgarte):** Resolves the penetration depth ($d$) by pushing the objects apart along the normal vector, preventing them from sinking into each other.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python - Sphere vs. Sphere)\n\n```python\nimport math\n\nclass ContactManifold:\n    def __init__(self, normal, depth, points):\n        self.normal = normal # (nx, ny)\n        self.depth = depth   # float\n        self.points = points # list of (x,y) contact points\n\nclass Sphere:\n    def __init__(self, x, y, r):\n        self.x = float(x)\n        self.y = float(y)\n        self.r = float(r)\n\ndef collide_spheres(sphere_a, sphere_b):\n    # 1. Distance vector between centers\n    dx = sphere_b.x - sphere_a.x\n    dy = sphere_b.y - sphere_a.y\n    dist = math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy)\n    \n    sum_radii = sphere_a.r + sphere_b.r\n    \n    # 2. Check for overlap\n    if dist >= sum_radii:\n        return None # No collision\n        \n    # 3. Generate Contact Manifold\n    # Normal points from A to B\n    nx = dx / dist if dist > 0.0 else 1.0\n    ny = dy / dist if dist > 0.0 else 0.0\n    \n    depth = sum_radii - dist\n    \n    # Contact point lies at the midpoint of overlap along the normal\n    contact_x = sphere_a.x + nx * (sphere_a.r - depth * 0.5)\n    contact_y = sphere_a.y + ny * (sphere_a.r - depth * 0.5)\n    \n    return ContactManifold((nx, ny), depth, [(contact_x, contact_y)])\n```\n",
