    "slug": "game-phys-broad-phase",
    "markdown": "# Game Physics: Broad Phase Collision Detection\n\nIn a game containing $N$ objects, a naive collision detection system checks every object against every other object. This **$O(N^2)$ check** scales poorly: with 1,000 entities, the engine performs 500,000 checks per frame, causing massive slowdowns.\n\nTo solve this, physics engines split collision detection into a two-phase pipeline:\n1.  **Broad Phase:** Filters out objects that are clearly too far apart to collide using fast, low-precision checks. This reduces candidate checks from $O(N^2)$ to $O(N \\log N)$ or average $O(N)$.\n2.  **Narrow Phase:** Runs precise, expensive geometric tests (like GJK or SAT) only on the candidates flagged by the broad phase.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Bounding Volume Approximations\n\nBefore performing spatial sorting, the broad phase wraps each object in a simple **Bounding Volume**—either a **Bounding Sphere** or an **Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB)**. \n\nChecking if two AABBs overlap is extremely fast, requiring only six scalar comparison checks. If the bounding volumes do not overlap, the narrow phase test is skipped entirely.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Spatial Partitioning Algorithms\n\nTo avoid comparing all bounding boxes, the broad phase organizes objects using **spatial indexes**:\n\n### 2.1 Sweep and Prune (SAP)\nSAP projects the bounding boxes of all objects onto a single axis (usually the X-axis) and sorts their min-max coordinates:\n\n```\nProjection Axis:\nObject A: [   ]\nObject B:    [   ]\nObject C:           [   ]\nSorted list: A_min, A_max, B_min, B_max, C_min, C_max\n```\n\nThe engine scans the sorted list. When it encounters a `min` bound, it adds the object to an active list, generating collision candidates with other active objects. It removes objects from the active list when it hits their `max` bound. \n\nBecause objects in games change positions slowly between frames (**temporal coherence**), sorting the list is extremely fast (using insertion sort, which runs in $O(N)$ time on nearly-sorted lists).\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Axis Line -->\n  <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"460\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-blue)\" />\n  <text x=\"410\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">X-Axis projection</text>\n  \n  <!-- AABB 1 -->\n  <rect x=\"60\" y=\"40\" width=\"120\" height=\"70\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"70\" y=\"60\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">AABB 1</text>\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <line x1=\"180\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"160\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"160\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  \n  <!-- AABB 2 (Overlapping AABB 1 on X projection) -->\n  <rect x=\"140\" y=\"80\" width=\"140\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"150\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">AABB 2</text>\n  <line x1=\"140\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <line x1=\"280\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"160\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"160\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  \n  <!-- Overlap segment -->\n  <rect x=\"140\" y=\"155\" width=\"40\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#ef4444\" fill-opacity=\"0.4\" />\n  <text x=\"135\" y=\"190\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">X-Overlap Candidate</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 Spatial Hashing\nSubdivides the game world into a grid of virtual cells. Each cell contains a list of objects overlapping it.\n\nTo find collision candidates:\n1.  Map each object's AABB coordinates to grid cell indices.\n2.  Use a hash function to map multi-dimensional cell coordinates $(x, y, z)$ to a single array index.\n3.  Only compare objects that map to the same hash cell.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Implementation Example: Spatial Grid Partitioning (Python)\n\n```python\nclass GridBroadPhase:\n    def __init__(self, cell_size=50.0):\n        self.cell_size = float(cell_size)\n        self.grid = {} # Map cell coords (cx, cy) to list of object IDs\n        \n    def _get_cell_coords(self, pos):\n        # Convert position coordinate to grid cell index\n        cx = int(pos[0] // self.cell_size)\n        cy = int(pos[1] // self.cell_size)\n        return (cx, cy)\n        \n    def insert_object(self, obj_id, pos, radius):\n        # Find which cells the bounding box overlaps\n        min_cell = self._get_cell_coords((pos[0] - radius, pos[1] - radius))\n        max_cell = self._get_cell_coords((pos[0] + radius, pos[1] + radius))\n        \n        # Add object to all overlapping grid cells\n        for cx in range(min_cell[0], max_cell[0] + 1):\n            for cy in range(min_cell[1], max_cell[1] + 1):\n                cell_key = (cx, cy)\n                if cell_key not in self.grid:\n                    self.grid[cell_key] = []\n                self.grid[cell_key].append(obj_id)\n                \n    def get_collision_candidates(self):\n        # Generate candidates from objects sharing the same grid cells\n        candidates = set()\n        for cell_key, obj_list in self.grid.items():\n            if len(obj_list) > 1:\n                # Compare all pairs within the cell\n                for i in range(len(obj_list)):\n                    for j in range(i + 1, len(obj_list)):\n                        pair = tuple(sorted((obj_list[i], obj_list[j])))\n                        candidates.add(pair)\n        return list(candidates)\n```\n",
    "title": "Broad Phase Detection",
    "tagline": "O(N^2) filtering, Sweep and Prune (SAP), and Spatial Hashing structures.",
