    "slug": "game-phys-obb",
    "markdown": "# Game Physics: Oriented Bounding Boxes (OBB)\n\nAn **Oriented Bounding Box (OBB)** is a rectangular volume that is aligned with the local coordinate system of its owner, rotating along with the object. \n\nUnlike an **Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB)**, which must expand to envelope a rotating object, an OBB maintains a tight fit, making it much more accurate for detecting collisions on elongated or rotated entities (like vehicles or weapons).\n\n---\n\n## 1. OBB Representation\n\nAn OBB in 3D space is mathematically defined by:\n\n*   **Center ($\\mathbf{c}$):** The 3D position vector defining the center of the box.\n*   **Local Axes ($\\mathbf{u}_1, \\mathbf{u}_2, \\mathbf{u}_3$):** Three mutually perpendicular unit vectors defining the local orientation of the box (columns of its rotation matrix).\n*   **Extents ($e_1, e_2, e_3$):** The half-widths of the box along each of its local axes.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- OBB rotated container -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(250,110) rotate(22)\">\n    <!-- OBB Box boundary -->\n    <rect x=\"-90\" y=\"-50\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.08\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n    \n    <!-- Center Point -->\n    <circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n    <text x=\"10\" y=\"-10\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">Center (c)</text>\n    \n    <!-- Axis 1 (u1) and extent e1 -->\n    <line x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"0\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-amber)\" />\n    <text x=\"45\" y=\"15\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">u1 (extent e1)</text>\n\n    <!-- Axis 2 (u2) and extent e2 -->\n    <line x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"-50\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-amber)\" />\n    <text x=\"5\" y=\"-30\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">u2 (extent e2)</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Collision Detection: Separating Axis Theorem (SAT)\n\nTo check for a collision between two OBBs (Box A and Box B), we use the **Separating Axis Theorem (SAT)**. The theorem states: *If you can project the two shapes onto a line and find a gap where their projections do not overlap, the shapes do not collide.*\n\nFor two OBBs in 3D, we must test **15 candidate axes** to guarantee a correct collision check:\n\n1.  The 3 local axes of Box A: $\\mathbf{u}_{A1}, \\mathbf{u}_{A2}, \\mathbf{u}_{A3}$\n2.  The 3 local axes of Box B: $\\mathbf{u}_{B1}, \\mathbf{u}_{B2}, \\mathbf{u}_{B3}$\n3.  The 9 cross products of their axes: $\\mathbf{u}_{Ai} \\times \\mathbf{u}_{Bj}$ (for $i, j \\in \\{1, 2, 3\\}$)\n\nIf there is an overlap along all 15 axes, the OBBs are colliding.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Projection Math on a Candidate Axis $\\mathbf{L}$\n\nTo project an OBB onto a candidate axis vector $\\mathbf{L}$, we calculate the maximum projection radius $R$ of the box relative to its center:\n\n$$R = e_1 |\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{L}| + e_2 |\\mathbf{u}_2 \\cdot \\mathbf{L}| + e_3 |\\mathbf{u}_3 \\cdot \\mathbf{L}|$$\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Axis L -->\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-blue)\" />\n  <text x=\"410\" y=\"170\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Test Axis L</text>\n  \n  <!-- Box A -->\n  <polygon points=\"100,50 180,90 140,130 60,90\" fill=\"#a855f7\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#a855f7\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <!-- Center of A -->\n  <circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"90\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#a855f7\" />\n  \n  <!-- Projections to Axis -->\n  <!-- Center projection -->\n  <line x1=\"120\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  \n  <!-- Min-Max projections -->\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <line x1=\"180\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  \n  <!-- Projected segment on L -->\n  <rect x=\"60\" y=\"146\" width=\"120\" height=\"8\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.4\" />\n  <circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"150\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  \n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"135\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Radius R</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nFor two boxes A and B, the distance between their projected centers is:\n\n$$D = |(\\mathbf{c}_B - \\mathbf{c}_A) \\cdot \\mathbf{L}|$$\n\nThe projections overlap along axis $\\mathbf{L}$ if and only if the distance between their centers is less than the sum of their projection radii:\n\n$$D < R_A + R_B$$\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python)\n\n```python\nclass OBB2D:\n    def __init__(self, center, axes, extents):\n        self.c = list(center)   # (x, y)\n        self.u = axes           # list of 2 orthogonal unit vectors [(ux, uy), (vx, vy)]\n        self.e = list(extents)  # (e1, e2) half-widths\n        \n    def get_projection_radius(self, axis):\n        # Project extents onto target axis: R = e1*|u1.L| + e2*|u2.L|\n        axis_x, axis_y = axis\n        r0 = self.e[0] * abs(self.u[0][0] * axis_x + self.u[0][1] * axis_y)\n        r1 = self.e[1] * abs(self.u[1][0] * axis_x + self.u[1][1] * axis_y)\n        return r0 + r1\n        \n    def collides_with(self, other):\n        # 2D SAT has 4 candidate axes: 2 local axes of A, 2 local axes of B\n        candidate_axes = [self.u[0], self.u[1], other.u[0], other.u[1]]\n        \n        for axis in candidate_axes:\n            # Distance between centers projected on axis\n            dx = other.c[0] - self.c[0]\n            dy = other.c[1] - self.c[1]\n            dist_proj = abs(dx * axis[0] + dy * axis[1])\n            \n            # Projection radii\n            r_a = self.get_projection_radius(axis)\n            r_b = other.get_projection_radius(axis)\n            \n            # If a separation is found along this axis, they do not overlap\n            if dist_proj > r_a + r_b:\n                return False\n        # Overlap along all axes -> Collision\n        return True\n```\n",
