    "slug": "game-phys-sat",
    "markdown": "# Game Physics: Separating Axis Theorem (SAT)\n\nThe **Separating Axis Theorem (SAT)** is a fundamental algorithm used in game physics to detect collisions between convex polygons (in 2D) and polyhedra (in 3D). \n\n---\n\n## 1. Core Principle\n\nThe theorem states:\n\n> *Two convex shapes do not overlap if and only if there exists a line (axis) upon which their projected shadows do not overlap.*\n\nIf we can find even a single axis where the projections of the two shapes are separated, we can instantly exit and declare **no collision**. This axis is called the **separating axis**. If no separating axis can be found across all candidate directions, the shapes are colliding.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Projection Math\n\nTo project a shape onto an axis defined by a unit vector $\\mathbf{L}$:\n1.  Project every vertex $\\mathbf{v}_i$ of the shape onto the axis using the dot product:\n    $$p_i = \\mathbf{v}_i \\cdot \\mathbf{L}$$\n2.  Find the minimum and maximum projection values to form an interval:\n    $$\\text{Interval} = [p_{\\text{min}}, p_{\\text{max}}]$$\n\nFor two shapes, A and B, their intervals $[p_{A,\\text{min}}, p_{A,\\text{max}}]$ and $[p_{B,\\text{min}}, p_{B,\\text{max}}]$ overlap if:\n\n$$p_{A,\\text{min}} \\le p_{B,\\text{max}} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad p_{B,\\text{min}} \\le p_{A,\\text{max}}$$\n\n---\n\n## 3. Finding Candidate Axes in 2D\n\nYou cannot test infinite random axes. For convex polygons, the only candidate axes you need to test are the **normals (perpendicular vectors) of the edges of both polygons**.\n\nFor a polygon with vertices $\\mathbf{v}_0, \\mathbf{v}_1, \\dots$:\n1.  Calculate each edge vector: $\\mathbf{e}_i = \\mathbf{v}_{i+1} - \\mathbf{v}_i$\n2.  Find the perpendicular normal vector: $\\mathbf{n}_i = (-e_{i,y}, e_{i,x})$\n3.  Normalize $\\mathbf{n}_i$ to obtain the unit test axis $\\mathbf{L}$.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Non-overlapping Case (Separated) -->\n  <!-- Axis L -->\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-blue)\" />\n  <text x=\"400\" y=\"200\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Separating Axis L</text>\n  \n  <!-- Polygon A (Triangle) -->\n  <polygon points=\"80,50 160,40 120,110\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"80\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <line x1=\"160\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <rect x=\"80\" y=\"176\" width=\"80\" height=\"8\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.4\" />\n  <text x=\"90\" y=\"170\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Proj A</text>\n  \n  <!-- Polygon B (Box - shifted right) -->\n  <polygon points=\"260,90 340,70 360,130 280,150\" fill=\"#a855f7\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#a855f7\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"260\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <line x1=\"360\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <rect x=\"260\" y=\"176\" width=\"100\" height=\"8\" fill=\"#a855f7\" fill-opacity=\"0.4\" />\n  <text x=\"280\" y=\"170\" fill=\"#a855f7\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Proj B</text>\n  \n  <!-- Gap marker -->\n  <line x1=\"160\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"3\" />\n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"165\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">GAP FOUND (No Collision)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 4. Resolving Collisions: The Minimum Translation Vector (MTV)\n\nIf overlaps occur along all axes, the shapes collide. The narrow phase then calculates the **Minimum Translation Vector (MTV)** to resolve the overlap.\n\n*   **Direction:** The axis that has the **smallest overlap depth**.\n*   **Magnitude:** The depth of overlap along that axis.\n\nBy shifting the objects apart along the MTV, the physics engine resolves the intersection in the most efficient direction.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implementation Example (Python)\n\n```python\nclass Polygon2D:\n    def __init__(self, vertices):\n        self.vertices = vertices # List of (x, y) tuples\n        \n    def get_axes(self):\n        axes = []\n        n = len(self.vertices)\n        for i in range(n):\n            p1 = self.vertices[i]\n            p2 = self.vertices[(i + 1) % n]\n            # Edge vector\n            edge_x = p2[0] - p1[0]\n            edge_y = p2[1] - p1[1]\n            # Normal vector perpendicular to edge\n            normal = (-edge_y, edge_x)\n            # Normalize\n            length = math.sqrt(normal[0]**2 + normal[1]**2)\n            if length > 0.0:\n                axes.append((normal[0] / length, normal[1] / length))\n        return axes\n        \n    def project(self, axis):\n        # Project all vertices onto the axis\n        dots = [v[0] * axis[0] + v[1] * axis[1] for v in self.vertices]\n        return min(dots), max(dots)\n\ndef check_collision_sat(poly_a, poly_b):\n    axes = poly_a.get_axes() + poly_b.get_axes()\n    min_overlap = float('inf')\n    mtv_axis = None\n    \n    for axis in axes:\n        min_a, max_a = poly_a.project(axis)\n        min_b, max_b = poly_b.project(axis)\n        \n        # Check separation interval\n        if max_a < min_b or max_b < min_a:\n            return None # Separation found, no collision\n            \n        # Calculate overlap depth\n        overlap = min(max_a, max_b) - max(min_a, min_b)\n        if overlap < min_overlap:\n            min_overlap = overlap\n            mtv_axis = axis\n            \n    # Return overlap depth and the direction vector (MTV)\n    return mtv_axis, min_overlap\n```\n",
    "title": "Separating Axis Theorem (SAT)",
