    "slug": "game-phys-convex",
    "markdown": "# Game Physics: Convex Shapes\n\nA **Convex Shape** is any geometric primitive or mesh where all interior points satisfy the convexity segment test. Because of their mathematical efficiency, game engines use convex shapes for almost all dynamic physics calculations.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Mathematical Representation\n\nConvex shapes are represented in physics engines in two ways:\n\n### 1.1 Convex Hull (Vertex Representation)\nA convex shape is defined as the convex combination of a set of boundary vertices $\\{\\mathbf{v}_i\\}$:\n\n$$\\mathbf{x} = \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\alpha_i \\mathbf{v}_i, \\quad \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\alpha_i = 1, \\quad \\alpha_i \\ge 0$$\n\nAny point inside the shape is a weighted average of these outer boundary coordinates.\n\n### 1.2 Half-Space Intersection (Plane Representation)\nA convex shape can be defined as the intersection of a set of boundary planes (half-spaces):\n\n$$\\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_i \\le d_i, \\quad \\text{for } i = 1, \\dots, K$$\n\nWhere $\\mathbf{n}_i$ is the outward-pointing normal of boundary plane $i$, and $d_i$ is its distance offset from the origin.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Planes wrapping to form shape -->\n  <!-- Plane 1 -->\n  <line x1=\"80\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n  <line x1=\"160\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rose)\" />\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"20\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">n1</text>\n  \n  <!-- Plane 2 -->\n  <line x1=\"240\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n  \n  <!-- Plane 3 -->\n  <line x1=\"320\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n  \n  <!-- Plane 4 -->\n  <line x1=\"120\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n  \n  <!-- Formed Convex Shape (Intersected zone) -->\n  <polygon points=\"160,40 240,30 320,140 120,170 80,50\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"160\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"13\">Convex Shape</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Common Convex Primitives & Support Mappings\n\nTo run GJK and EPA, an object only needs to define a **Support Function** (`get_furthest_point(d)`). This allows us to simulate smooth primitives without storing thousands of vertices:\n\n*   **Sphere (Radius $R$, Center $\\mathbf{c}$):**\n    $$s(\\mathbf{d}) = \\mathbf{c} + R \\frac{\\mathbf{d}}{\\|\\mathbf{d}\\|}$$\n*   **Capsule (Radius $R$, Line Segment endpoints $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$):**\n    Find which segment endpoint is furthest along direction $\\mathbf{d}$, then expand along $\\mathbf{d}$ by radius $R$:\n    $$s(\\mathbf{d}) = \\text{if } (\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{d} > \\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{d}) \\text{ then } \\mathbf{a} \\text{ else } \\mathbf{b} + R \\frac{\\mathbf{d}}{\\|\\mathbf{d}\\|}$$\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 160\" width=\"100%\" height=\"160\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Capsule Core Segment -->\n  <line x1=\"160\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"80\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <text x=\"145\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Point a</text>\n  \n  <circle cx=\"340\" cy=\"80\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <text x=\"330\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Point b</text>\n  \n  <!-- Capsule Shell (Radius swept) -->\n  <path d=\"M 160 40 L 340 40 A 40 40 0 0 1 340 120 L 160 120 A 40 40 0 0 1 160 40 Z\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"140\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">Capsule Primitive</text>\n  \n  <!-- Radius indicator -->\n  <line x1=\"160\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <text x=\"165\" y=\"60\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">R</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Implementation Example (Python - Capsule Support Mapping)\n\n```python\nimport math\n\nclass CapsulePrimitive:\n    def __init__(self, point_a, point_b, radius):\n        self.a = list(point_a)   # (x, y)\n        self.b = list(point_b)   # (x, y)\n        self.r = float(radius)\n        \n    def get_furthest_point(self, direction):\n        # 1. Normalize the search direction\n        dir_x, dir_y = direction\n        length = math.sqrt(dir_x**2 + dir_y**2)\n        dx, dy = dir_x / length, dir_y / length\n        \n        # 2. Find which segment endpoint lies furthest along direction\n        dot_a = self.a[0] * dx + self.a[1] * dy\n        dot_b = self.b[0] * dx + self.b[1] * dy\n        \n        base_point = self.a if dot_a > dot_b else self.b\n        \n        # 3. Project outward along direction by radius r\n        support_x = base_point[0] + dx * self.r\n        support_y = base_point[1] + dy * self.r\n        \n        return (support_x, support_y)\n\n# Test: Horizontal capsule from (0,0) to (5,0) with radius 2\ncapsule = CapsulePrimitive((0.0, 0.0), (5.0, 0.0), 2.0)\n# Search direction is right (1, 0) -> Should return (5 + 2, 0) = (7.0, 0.0)\nprint(\"Support point along (1,0):\", capsule.get_furthest_point((1.0, 0.0)))\n```\n",
