    "slug": "game-phys-convex-hull",
    "markdown": "# Game Physics: Convex Hull Algorithms\n\nA **Convex Hull** is the smallest convex boundary that completely encloses a set of points. Think of it as stretching a rubber band around a collection of nails on a board; when released, the shape of the rubber band defines the convex hull.\n\nIn games, we use convex hulls to generate optimized collision boundaries from complex 3D meshes (like character models or environment items).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Jarvis March (Gift Wrapping)\n\n**Jarvis March** is an intuitive algorithm to construct a convex hull. \n\n### Algorithm Steps:\n1.  Find the leftmost point $\\mathbf{P}_0$ (guaranteed to be on the hull).\n2.  Search for the next point $\\mathbf{P}_{i+1}$ such that all other points lie to the left of the directed line $\\mathbf{P}_i \\rightarrow \\mathbf{P}_{i+1}$. This is verified using the cross product:\n    $$(\\mathbf{P}_{i+1} - \\mathbf{P}_i) \\times (\\mathbf{Q} - \\mathbf{P}_i) \\ge 0 \\quad \\text{for all points }\\mathbf{Q}$$\n3.  Set the new point as $\\mathbf{P}_i$ and repeat the scan.\n4.  Exit when the loop returns to the starting point $\\mathbf{P}_0$.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Point Cloud -->\n  <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"110\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">P0 (Start)</text>\n  \n  <circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"60\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" />\n  <circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"110\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" />\n  <circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"50\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" />\n  <circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"140\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" />\n  \n  <circle cx=\"340\" cy=\"60\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <text x=\"330\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">P1</text>\n  \n  <circle cx=\"380\" cy=\"130\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <text x=\"375\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">P2</text>\n  \n  <!-- Current Wrapping edge P0 -> P1 -->\n  <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" />\n  \n  <!-- Next candidate sweep line P1 -> P2 -->\n  <line x1=\"340\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n  \n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Gift Wrapping: O(n * h) Complexity</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n*   **Complexity:** $O(nh)$, where $n$ is the total number of points and $h$ is the number of vertices on the final hull.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Graham Scan\n\n**Graham Scan** is a highly efficient algorithm that runs in $O(n \\log n)$ time.\n\n### Algorithm Steps:\n1.  Find the point $\\mathbf{P}$ with the lowest Y-coordinate.\n2.  Sort all remaining points by the **polar angle** they make with $\\mathbf{P}$.\n3.  Initialize a stack containing the first three sorted points.\n4.  For each subsequent point:\n    *   Evaluate if the turn made from the second-to-last stack point, to the last stack point, to the current point is a **counter-clockwise turn (left turn)**.\n    *   If it is a clockwise turn (right turn), the intermediate point lies inside the hull boundary. Pop it off the stack and repeat the check.\n    *   Once a left turn is verified, push the current point onto the stack.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Quickhull\n\n**Quickhull** is a divide-and-conquer algorithm similar to Quicksort.\n\n1.  Find the points with the minimum and maximum X-coordinates (guaranteed to be on the hull). Draw a line between them, splitting the point cloud into two sets.\n2.  For each set, find the point furthest from the dividing line. This point forms a triangle with the ends of the line.\n3.  Points inside the triangle are discarded, as they cannot lie on the hull.\n4.  Repeat the process recursively on the outside edges of the triangle.\n*   **Complexity:** Average $O(n \\log n)$, worst-case $O(n^2)$ if the points are arranged in a circle.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example: Graham Scan (Python)\n\n```python\ndef polar_angle_sort_key(p, origin):\n    # Calculate angle and distance for sorting\n    dx = p[0] - origin[0]\n    dy = p[1] - origin[1]\n    return math.atan2(dy, dx), dx*dx + dy*dy\n\ndef cross_product_turn(p1, p2, p3):\n    # Returns positive for left turn (CCW), negative for right turn, 0 for collinear\n    return (p2[0] - p1[0]) * (p3[1] - p2[1]) - (p2[1] - p1[1]) * (p3[0] - p2[0])\n\ndef compute_convex_hull_graham(points):\n    if len(points) < 3:\n        return points\n        \n    # 1. Find point with lowest Y coordinate (then leftmost X coordinate)\n    origin = min(points, key=lambda p: (p[1], p[0]))\n    \n    # 2. Sort remaining points by polar angle relative to origin\n    remaining = [p for p in points if p != origin]\n    remaining.sort(key=lambda p: polar_angle_sort_key(p, origin))\n    \n    # 3. Build Hull stack\n    hull = [origin, remaining[0], remaining[1]]\n    \n    for p in remaining[2:]:\n        # Pop points off the stack that create a right-hand turn\n        while len(hull) >= 2 and cross_product_turn(hull[-2], hull[-1], p) <= 0:\n            hull.pop()\n        hull.append(p)\n        \n    return hull\n\n# Test case\npts = [(0, 3), (1, 1), (2, 2), (4, 4), (0, 0), (1, 2), (3, 1), (3, 3)]\nhull = compute_convex_hull_graham(pts)\nprint(\"Convex Hull Vertices:\", hull)\n```\n",
    "title": "Convex Hull Algorithms",
    "tagline": "Gift Wrapping (Jarvis March), Graham Scan stack, and Quickhull recursion.",
    "category": "Game Developer",
