    "slug": "game-phys-convex-dec",
    "markdown": "# Game Physics: Convex Decomposition\n\nWhile game designers model rich, concave assets (like hollow rooms, complex weapons, or vehicles), physics solvers require convex shapes to perform high-speed collision updates. To bridge this gap, we use **Convex Decomposition** to subdivide a concave 3D mesh into a compound union of smaller convex hulls.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Algorithmic Approaches\n\nThere are two primary ways to partition a concave shape:\n\n### 1.1 Exact Convex Decomposition (ECD)\nAttempts to split the shape into the absolute minimum number of convex parts without changing the boundary volume. \n*   **Problem:** This is an **NP-hard** problem. Computing it for complex 3D meshes takes hours, making it completely impractical for game production pipelines.\n\n### 1.2 Approximate Convex Decomposition (ACD)\nAllows the decomposed parts to be *slightly* different from the original mesh, within a user-defined error threshold. \n*   By allowing small approximations (e.g. filling in tiny indentations), ACD runs extremely fast and reduces the total number of convex colliders, saving runtime memory and CPU cycles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. V-HACD (Volumetric Hierarchical ACD)\n\nThe industry standard algorithm for convex decomposition is **V-HACD** (Volumetric Hierarchical Approximate Convex Decomposition), which is integrated directly into engines like Unreal Engine, Unity, and Godot.\n\nV-HACD operates on 3D meshes using a voxel-based approach:\n\n```\n[Concave Mesh] ──> Voxelize Volume ──> Find Best Split Planes ──> Generate Convex Hulls\n```\n\n### The V-HACD Process:\n1.  **Voxelization:** Converts the 3D triangle mesh into a dense grid of 3D voxels (like Minecraft blocks) to define the internal volume.\n2.  **Split Plane Search:** Searches for a plane that splits the voxel volume into two sub-volumes such that the resulting concavity (empty space inside the convex hulls) is minimized.\n3.  **Recursion:** Repeats the splitting process recursively on the sub-volumes until the concavity error falls below the user's target threshold.\n4.  **Hull Generation:** Computes the convex hull for the voxels in each leaf node, yielding the final compound collider.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Step 1: Concave Donut (Mesh) -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(10, 20)\">\n    <path d=\"M 30 80 A 40 40 0 1 1 110 80 A 40 40 0 1 1 30 80 Z\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n    <circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"80\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#141416\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n    <text x=\"15\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">1. Concave Mesh</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  <!-- Arrow 1 -->\n  <path d=\"M 140 100 L 175 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#a1a1aa\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-zinc)\" />\n  \n  <!-- Step 2: Voxelized representation -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(160, 20)\">\n    <!-- Simulated voxel boxes wrapping the donut -->\n    <rect x=\"25\" y=\"55\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.2\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n    <rect x=\"45\" y=\"35\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.2\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n    <rect x=\"65\" y=\"35\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.2\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n    <rect x=\"85\" y=\"55\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.2\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n    <rect x=\"85\" y=\"75\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.2\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n    <rect x=\"65\" y=\"95\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.2\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n    <rect x=\"45\" y=\"95\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.2\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n    <rect x=\"25\" y=\"75\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.2\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n    <text x=\"15\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">2. Voxelized Grid</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  <!-- Arrow 2 -->\n  <path d=\"M 290 100 L 325 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#a1a1aa\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-zinc)\" />\n  \n  <!-- Step 3: Decomposed hulls -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(320, 20)\">\n    <!-- 4 split convex hulls forming the torus -->\n    <polygon points=\"15,65 45,35 70,55 35,90\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n    <polygon points=\"65,35 105,35 115,75 70,55\" fill=\"#10b981\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n    <polygon points=\"115,75 105,115 65,115 70,85\" fill=\"#a855f7\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#a855f7\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n    <polygon points=\"65,115 25,115 15,75 55,85\" fill=\"#e879f9\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#e879f9\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n    <text x=\"5\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">3. Convex Hulls</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Implementation Example: Split Plane Valuation (Python)\n\nHere is a Python simulator demonstrating how a decomposition algorithm evaluates a candidate split plane by measuring the concavity error of the resulting halves:\n\n```python\nclass VolumePartition:\n    def __init__(self, voxels):\n        self.voxels = voxels # List of (x, y, z) voxel coordinates\n        \n    def calculate_concavity_volume(self, hull_points):\n        # Approximates empty space inside the convex hull\n        # concavity = volume(convex_hull) - volume(voxels)\n        hull_volume = calculate_convex_hull_volume(hull_points)\n        voxel_volume = len(self.voxels) # Each voxel is 1 unit\n        return max(0.0, hull_volume - voxel_volume)\n        \n    def evaluate_split_plane(self, plane_coordinate, axis='x'):\n        left_voxels = []\n        right_voxels = []\n        \n        # 1. Split voxels into two groups\n        for v in self.voxels:\n            val = v[0] if axis == 'x' else (v[1] if axis == 'y' else v[2])\n            if val < plane_coordinate:\n                left_voxels.append(v)\n            else:\n                right_voxels.append(v)\n                \n        # 2. Compute convex hull concavity for each side\n        left_hull = compute_convex_hull_3d(left_voxels)\n        right_hull = compute_convex_hull_3d(right_voxels)\n        \n        left_error = self.calculate_concavity_volume(left_hull)\n        right_error = self.calculate_concavity_volume(right_hull)\n        \n        # 3. Total error\n        total_error = left_error + right_error\n        return total_error # Lower error = better split plane\n```\n",
    "title": "Convex Decomposition",
