    "slug": "game-phys-dbvt",
    "markdown": "# Game Physics: Dynamic Bounding Volume Trees (DBVT)\n\nA **Dynamic Bounding Volume Tree (DBVT)** is a self-balancing binary tree where leaf nodes represent object bounding volumes (like AABBs) and parent nodes represent the union bounding volumes of their children. \n\nDBVT is widely used in physics engines (like Box2D, Bullet, and PhysX) for both the **Broad Phase** of collision detection and fast spatial queries (like raycasts and frustum culling).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Tree Architecture\n\nA DBVT organizes bounding boxes hierarchically:\n\n```\n          [ Node A ]  (Root - wraps all shapes)\n          /        \\\n    [ Node B ]      [ Node C ] (wraps leaves 3 & 4)\n     /      \\          /     \\\n  Leaf 1   Leaf 2   Leaf 3  Leaf 4\n```\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 240\" width=\"100%\" height=\"240\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Root bounding box (A) -->\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"30\" width=\"440\" height=\"180\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Root A</text>\n  \n  <!-- Left Child (B - wraps 1 & 2) -->\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"60\" width=\"200\" height=\"140\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Node B</text>\n  \n  <!-- Leaf 1 -->\n  <rect x=\"50\" y=\"90\" width=\"80\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n  <text x=\"60\" y=\"110\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Leaf 1</text>\n  \n  <!-- Leaf 2 -->\n  <rect x=\"140\" y=\"110\" width=\"90\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n  <text x=\"150\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Leaf 2</text>\n  \n  <!-- Right Child (C - wraps 3 & 4) -->\n  <rect x=\"260\" y=\"60\" width=\"200\" height=\"140\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Node C</text>\n  \n  <!-- Leaf 3 -->\n  <rect x=\"270\" y=\"90\" width=\"80\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n  <text x=\"280\" y=\"110\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Leaf 3</text>\n  \n  <!-- Leaf 4 -->\n  <rect x=\"360\" y=\"90\" width=\"90\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n  <text x=\"370\" y=\"110\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Leaf 4</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nTo run a query (e.g. check which objects overlap a ray):\n1.  Check if the ray intersects the Root AABB (Node A).\n2.  If yes, check Node B and Node C.\n3.  Recursively traverse down the tree, skipping branches whose bounding boxes do not overlap.\n4.  This reduces search complexity to **$O(\\log N)$**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Dynamic Updates: Fat AABBs\n\nIn a dynamic game world, objects move continuously. Modifying the tree structure on every minor movement is computationally expensive.\n\nTo minimize tree rebuilds, engines use **Fat AABBs** (also called *AABB Slack*). \n\n*   The engine expands the actual bounding box of each leaf object by a padding margin (slack) in all directions.\n*   The leaf's position in the tree is only updated when the object moves outside this expanded \"fat\" boundary.\n*   Minor movements inside the padding boundary bypass tree updates entirely.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Tree Balancing & Rotations\n\nWhen an object moves outside its fat AABB, the engine removes its leaf node and reinserts it at its new position. Reinserting nodes can cause the binary tree to become unbalanced (degraded to $O(N)$ depth).\n\nTo prevent this, the engine runs **Tree Rotations** (similar to AVL tree or Red-Black tree rotations) during insertions and removals. It shifts branches to minimize the total **Surface Area Heuristic (SAH)**, which is the sum of the surface areas of all parent nodes in the tree. Minimizing the surface area keeps the tree tightly bounded and balanced.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python - Node representation)\n\n```python\nclass DBVTNode:\n    def __init__(self, aabb=None, leaf_id=None):\n        self.aabb = aabb       # AABB bounding box object\n        self.leaf_id = leaf_id # None for parent nodes, ID for leaves\n        self.parent = None\n        self.left = None\n        self.right = None\n        \n    def is_leaf(self):\n        return self.leaf_id is not None\n        \n    def update_aabb(self):\n        # Union parent's bounding box to envelop children\n        if self.is_leaf():\n            return\n        \n        min_x = min(self.left.aabb.min[0], self.right.aabb.min[0])\n        min_y = min(self.left.aabb.min[1], self.right.aabb.min[1])\n        max_x = max(self.left.aabb.max[0], self.right.aabb.max[0])\n        max_y = max(self.left.aabb.max[1], self.right.aabb.max[1])\n        \n        self.aabb.min = (min_x, min_y)\n        self.aabb.max = (max_x, max_y)\n```\n",
    "title": "Dynamic Bounding Volume Trees (DBVT)",
    "tagline": "Self-balancing binary trees, fat AABBs, and tree rotation optimizations.",
