    "slug": "game-gfx-sampling",
    "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Sampling & Aliasing\n\nIn computer graphics, we convert continuous geometric shapes, lighting signals, and textures into a discrete grid of pixels. This process of converting a continuous signal into discrete values is called **Sampling**. \n\nIf we sample a signal too slowly, we introduce mathematical distortions called **Aliasing** (visible as jagged edges or flickering textures).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Signal Processing Theory & The Nyquist Limit\n\nA digital image is a 2D grid of color samples. To understand aliasing, we look to the **Nyquist-Shannon Sampling Theorem**:\n\n> *To accurately reconstruct a continuous signal without distortion (aliasing), the sampling frequency $f_s$ must be greater than twice the highest frequency component $f_{\\text{max}}$ present in the signal.*\n\n$$f_s > 2 f_{\\text{max}}$$\n\nThe threshold $f_N = \\frac{f_s}{2}$ is called the **Nyquist Frequency**. \n\nIf a signal contains frequencies higher than the Nyquist limit, those high frequencies fold back into the lower spectrum, masquerading as low-frequency patterns. In graphics, this results in **aliasing**:\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- High Frequency original wave -->\n  <path d=\"M 50 100 Q 62.5 40 75 100 Q 87.5 160 100 100 Q 112.5 40 125 100 Q 137.5 160 150 100 Q 162.5 40 175 100 Q 187.5 160 200 100 Q 212.5 40 225 100 Q 237.5 160 250 100 Q 262.5 40 275 100 Q 287.5 160 300 100 Q 312.5 40 325 100 Q 337.5 160 350 100 Q 362.5 40 375 100 Q 387.5 160 400 100 Q 412.5 40 425 100 Q 437.5 160 450 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  \n  <!-- Under-sampled points (Nyquist violated) -->\n  <!-- We sample at intervals of 50px: 50, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350, 400, 450 -->\n  <!-- Let's offset the wave slightly so samples hit peaks/troughs -->\n  <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n  <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n  <circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n  <circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n  <circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n  <circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n  <circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n  <circle cx=\"400\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n  <circle cx=\"450\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n  \n  <!-- Reconstructed low frequency wave (straight flat line or slow drift if sample offsets differ) -->\n  <!-- If we sample slightly off-phase, we get a false slow wave -->\n  <!-- Let's show a slow wave mapping these sample points -->\n  <path d=\"M 50 100 Q 150 180 250 100 Q 350 20 450 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" />\n  \n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Original High-Frequency Signal (Gray)</text>\n  <text x=\"90\" y=\"185\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">Aliased Reconstructed Signal (Rose)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Types of Aliasing in Graphics\n\n1.  **Spatial Aliasing (Jaggies):** High-frequency geometry edges crossing pixels abruptly, creating stair-step jagged lines on edges.\n2.  **Texture Aliasing (Moiré Patterns):** High-density textures rendered at a distance. When the pixel spacing is larger than the details of the texture pattern, swirling geometric noise appears.\n3.  **Temporal Aliasing (Wagon-Wheel Effect):** High-speed motion captured at discrete frame rates (e.g. car wheels appearing to spin backward, or fine textures flickering when the camera moves).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Anti-Aliasing (AA) Techniques\n\nTo minimize aliasing, engines use various Anti-Aliasing algorithms:\n\n### 3.1 Super-Sampling Anti-Aliasing (SSAA)\nRenders the game internally at a higher resolution (e.g. $4\\text{K}$) and downscales the output to fit the screen ($1080\\text{p}$).\n*   **Aesthetics:** High quality, removes all jaggies and texture noise.\n*   **Performance Cost:** Extremely expensive; requires rendering 4x the fragments, which is rarely viable for modern games.\n\n### 3.2 Multi-Sample Anti-Aliasing (MSAA)\nRuns the fragment shader once per pixel, but performs depth and coverage checks at multiple sub-pixel samples.\n*   **Aesthetics:** Smooths geometry edges cleanly.\n*   **Performance Cost:** Moderate; does not smooth transparency textures (like foliage grids) or shader calculations.\n\n### 3.3 Fast Approximate Anti-Aliasing (FXAA)\nA post-processing filter that runs in screen space, searching for high-contrast edges and blending adjacent pixels.\n*   **Aesthetics:** Can cause slight image blurriness.\n*   **Performance Cost:** Low, making it ideal for low-end platforms.\n\n### 3.4 Temporal Anti-Aliasing (TAA)\nAccumulates samples over consecutive frames. The projection matrix is jittered slightly by sub-pixel offsets in each frame, and the engine uses **Motion Vectors** to trace and blend pixels over time.\n*   **Aesthetics:** Smooths all geometry and shader aliasing cleanly, though fast movements can create ghosting trails.\n*   **Performance Cost:** Low, making it the industry standard for modern games.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python - SSAA Downscaling)\n\n```python\nclass ImageBuffer:\n    def __init__(self, width, height):\n        self.width = width\n        self.height = height\n        # Initialize grid with black pixels (r, g, b)\n        self.pixels = [[[0, 0, 0] for _ in range(width)] for _ in range(height)]\n\ndef resolve_ssaa_2x(supersampled_buffer):\n    # Downsamples a 2x SSAO buffer (e.g. 2000x2000 -> 1000x1000)\n    # Blends 4 sub-pixels to compute 1 output pixel color\n    out_w = supersampled_buffer.width // 2\n    out_h = supersampled_buffer.height // 2\n    output = ImageBuffer(out_w, out_h)\n    \n    for y in range(out_h):\n        for x in range(out_w):\n            # Coordinates of the 4 sub-pixels\n            s_x0, s_y0 = x * 2, y * 2\n            s_x1, s_y1 = s_x0 + 1, s_y0 + 1\n            \n            p00 = supersampled_buffer.pixels[s_y0][s_x0]\n            p10 = supersampled_buffer.pixels[s_y0][s_x1]\n            p01 = supersampled_buffer.pixels[s_y1][s_x0]\n            p11 = supersampled_buffer.pixels[s_y1][s_x1]\n            \n            # Average color components\n            r = (p00[0] + p10[0] + p01[0] + p11[0]) // 4\n            g = (p00[1] + p10[1] + p01[1] + p11[1]) // 4\n            b = (p00[2] + p10[2] + p01[2] + p11[2]) // 4\n            \n            output.pixels[y][x] = [r, g, b]\n            \n    return output\n```\n",
    "title": "Sampling & Aliasing",
    "tagline": "Signal processing, Nyquist-Shannon sampling limits, and spatial/temporal anti-aliasing (SSAA, MSAA, TAA).",
    "category": "Game Developer",
