    "slug": "game-gfx-reflection",
    "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Light Reflection Models\n\nLight reflection models define how surface materials interact with light sources to generate color and highlights. In real-time rendering, we use empirical models (like **Phong** and **Blinn-Phong**) for basic materials, and physically-based models (like **Cook-Torrance**) for high-end rendering.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Lambertian Reflection (Perfect Diffuse)\n\nLambertian reflection models ideal matte (non-shiny) surfaces (such as chalk or paper). It states that the brightness of a point is independent of the viewer's angle, depending only on the angle between the surface normal $\\mathbf{N}$ and the light direction $\\mathbf{L}$:\n\n$$I_{\\text{diffuse}} = I_{\\text{light}} \\cdot k_d \\max(\\mathbf{N} \\cdot \\mathbf{L}, 0)$$\n\nWhere $k_d$ is the diffuse reflection coefficient (surface color albedo). This relationship is called **Lambert's Cosine Law**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Phong Reflection Model (Diffuse + Specular)\n\nIntroduced by Bui Tuong Phong in 1975, this model adds a **Specular Highlight** to simulate shiny materials. Specular reflection depends on the angle between the viewer's direction $\\mathbf{V}$ and the mirror reflection direction $\\mathbf{R}$:\n\n$$\\mathbf{R} = 2(\\mathbf{N} \\cdot \\mathbf{L})\\mathbf{N} - \\mathbf{L}$$\n$$I_{\\text{specular}} = I_{\\text{light}} \\cdot k_s \\max(\\mathbf{V} \\cdot \\mathbf{R}, 0)^\\alpha$$\n\nWhere:\n*   $k_s$ is the specular reflection coefficient.\n*   $\\alpha$ is the **shininess exponent** (larger values create smaller, sharper highlights).\n\n---\n\n## 3. The Blinn-Phong Reflection Model\n\nJim Blinn updated the Phong model in 1977. Instead of calculating the expensive reflection vector $\\mathbf{R}$, Blinn-Phong calculates the **Halfway Vector ($\\mathbf{H}$)** between the light direction $\\mathbf{L}$ and view direction $\\mathbf{V}$:\n\n$$\\mathbf{H} = \\frac{\\mathbf{L} + \\mathbf{V}}{\\|\\mathbf{L} + \\mathbf{V}\\|}$$\n\nThe specular intensity is then calculated using the dot product of the normal $\\mathbf{N}$ and the halfway vector $\\mathbf{H}$:\n\n$$I_{\\text{specular}} = I_{\\text{light}} \\cdot k_s \\max(\\mathbf{N} \\cdot \\mathbf{H}, 0)^\\beta$$\n\nWhere $\\beta$ is adjusted to match Phong's shininess (typically $\\beta \\approx 4\\alpha$).\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Surface Plane -->\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  \n  <!-- Normal vector N -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-blue)\" />\n  <text x=\"260\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">N (Normal)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Light Vector L -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-amber)\" />\n  <text x=\"135\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">L (Light)</text>\n  \n  <!-- View Vector V -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"350\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-emerald)\" />\n  <text x=\"355\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">V (View)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Halfway Vector H (Blinn-Phong) -->\n  <!-- H lies exactly between L and V, i.e. along normal N in this symmetric case -->\n  <!-- Let's offset view to make it asymmetric and show H between them -->\n  <!-- If L = (-100, -100), V = (150, -100). H is between them -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"275\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"#a855f7\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-purple)\" />\n  <text x=\"285\" y=\"65\" fill=\"#a855f7\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">H (Halfway)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n### Advantages of Blinn-Phong:\n1.  **Computational Efficiency:** If the light source and camera are far from the surface (approximating directional lights and orthographic cameras), the halfway vector $\\mathbf{H}$ is constant across the entire mesh, allowing it to be computed once per draw call.\n2.  **No Back-Face Glitches:** In the Phong model, if the angle between the view vector and reflection vector exceeds $90^\\circ$, the dot product becomes negative, causing specular highlights to cut off abruptly. Blinn-Phong's dot product $(\\mathbf{N} \\cdot \\mathbf{H})$ remains positive and decays smoothly.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (GLSL Fragment Shader)\n\n```glsl\n#version 330 core\nout vec4 FragColor;\n\nin vec3 FragPos;\nin vec3 Normal;\n\nuniform vec3 lightPos;\nuniform vec3 viewPos;\nuniform vec3 lightColor;\nuniform vec3 objectColor;\n\nvoid main()\n{\n    // Vectors normalization\n    vec3 norm = normalize(Normal);\n    vec3 lightDir = normalize(lightPos - FragPos);\n    vec3 viewDir = normalize(viewPos - FragPos);\n    \n    // 1. Diffuse (Lambertian)\n    float diff = max(dot(norm, lightDir), 0.0);\n    vec3 diffuse = diff * lightColor;\n    \n    // 2. Specular (Blinn-Phong)\n    vec3 halfwayDir = normalize(lightDir + viewDir);\n    float spec = pow(max(dot(norm, halfwayDir), 0.0), 32.0); // 32.0 is shininess exponent\n    vec3 specular = spec * lightColor;\n    \n    // 3. Final color assembly\n    vec3 result = (diffuse + specular) * objectColor;\n    FragColor = vec4(result, 1.0);\n}\n```\n",
    "title": "Light Reflection Models",
    "tagline": "Lambertian diffuse reflection, Phong specular highlights, and Blinn-Phong halfway optimizations.",
    "category": "Game Developer",
    "readTime": 15,
