    "slug": "game-gfx-specular",
    "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Specular Reflection\n\n**Specular Reflection** is the direct, mirror-like reflection of light rays off a surface. It creates highlights on shiny materials (like glass, metals, and wet surfaces), where the light is reflected primarily in a single direction rather than scattered uniformly.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Fresnel Effect\n\nA key physical property of specular reflection is the **Fresnel Effect**: the amount of light reflected depends on the angle at which you view the surface. \n\n*   **Viewing Perpendicularly (Normal Incidence):** You see less reflection, allowing you to see through the surface (e.g. looking straight down into clear water).\n*   **Viewing at a Grazing Angle:** The surface becomes highly reflective, acting like a mirror (e.g. looking at water in the distance near the horizon).\n\nTo calculate this in real-time shaders, we use **Schlick’s Approximation**:\n\n$$R(\\theta) = R_0 + (1 - R_0)(1 - \\cos\\theta)^5$$\n\nWhere:\n*   $R_0$ is the base reflectance of the material at normal incidence (determined by its refractive index).\n*   $\\theta$ is the angle between the view vector and the surface normal: $\\cos\\theta = \\mathbf{V} \\cdot \\mathbf{N}$.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Grid Axes -->\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"440\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  \n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"30\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Reflectance R(θ)</text>\n  <text x=\"380\" y=\"195\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Angle θ (0° to 90°)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Base Reflectance R0 line -->\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"440\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <text x=\"70\" y=\"145\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Base R0 (e.g. 0.04 for Water)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Fresnel Curve (Exponential increase to 1.0 at 90 degrees) -->\n  <!-- Curve starting at (60, 150) and bending up to (440, 50) -->\n  <path d=\"M 60 150 Q 280 150 440 50\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"3\" />\n  \n  <!-- Labels -->\n  <circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"150\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <circle cx=\"440\" cy=\"50\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n  <text x=\"375\" y=\"40\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"10\">100% Reflective at 90°</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Microfacet Theory (PBR Specular)\n\nIn modern Physically Based Rendering (PBR) systems, specular highlights are calculated using **Microfacet Theory**. We assume that at a microscopic level, surfaces are composed of tiny, mirror-like flat facets (microfacets).\n\n*   A perfectly smooth surface has aligned microfacets, producing a sharp, mirrored highlight.\n*   A rough surface has randomly oriented microfacets, scattering light in a wider cone and producing a broader, softer highlight.\n\nThe PBR specular term is calculated using the Cook-Torrance model:\n\n$$f_{\\text{specular}} = \\frac{D \\cdot F \\cdot G}{4 (\\mathbf{V} \\cdot \\mathbf{N})(\\mathbf{L} \\cdot \\mathbf{N})}$$\n\nWhere:\n*   **$D$ (Normal Distribution Function):** Calculates the concentration of microfacets aligned with the halfway vector $\\mathbf{H}$ (determines highlight size).\n*   **$F$ (Fresnel Term):** Calculates reflectance changes based on the view angle.\n*   **$G$ (Geometry Shadowing/Masking Function):** Calculates how much light is blocked or shadowed by neighboring microfacets.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Specular and Roughness Maps\n\nTo define varying material properties across a single mesh:\n*   **Specular Map:** An RGB texture defining the specular color reflection (non-metals have monochrome values, while metals reflect color highlights, e.g. gold reflects gold highlights).\n*   **Roughness (or Glossiness) Map:** A grayscale texture defining how rough the microfacets are at each pixel, directly scaling the Normal Distribution Function $D$.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (GLSL Fresnel Calculation)\n\nHere is a GLSL shader function calculating Schlick's approximation for Fresnel reflectance:\n\n```glsl\nvec3 calculateFresnelSchlick(float cosTheta, vec3 F0)\n{\n    // F(theta) = F0 + (1 - F0) * (1 - cos(theta))^5\n    // F0: base reflectance color vector\n    return F0 + (vec3(1.0) - F0) * pow(clamp(1.0 - cosTheta, 0.0, 1.0), 5.0);\n}\n\nvoid main()\n{\n    vec3 N = normalize(Normal);\n    vec3 V = normalize(viewPos - FragPos);\n    \n    // Base specular reflectance for dielectric (non-metal) typical value: 0.04\n    vec3 F0 = vec3(0.04); \n    \n    // Calculate Fresnel reflectance multiplier\n    vec3 F = calculateFresnelSchlick(max(dot(N, V), 0.0), F0);\n    \n    // ... use F to weight specular vs diffuse contribution ...\n}\n```\n",
    "title": "Specular Reflection",
    "tagline": "Mirror-like light reflection, the Fresnel effect, Schlick's approximation, and microfacet BRDFs.",
    "category": "Game Developer",
    "readTime": 15,
    "lastUpdated": "July 2026",
