    "slug": "game-gfx-parallax",
    "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Parallax Mapping\n\nWhile normal mapping creates realistic lighting over bumps, it is flat: if you view a normal-mapped surface at a sharp angle, the bumps have no thickness and do not occlude each other. To solve this limitation, we use **Parallax Mapping**.\n\nParallax mapping shifts the texture coordinates at each pixel based on the camera's view angle and a **Height Map** texture, simulating physical depth, self-occlusion, and parallax offsets.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Core Principle & Coordinate Shifting\n\nWhen looking at a bumpy surface, the camera's view ray hits a point on the physical height field that does not align with the flat triangle plane. \n\nTo simulate this depth:\n1.  The shader samples the height map at the initial UV coordinate.\n2.  It uses the height value ($h$) and the view vector $\\mathbf{V}$ in tangent space to calculate a coordinate offset $\\Delta \\mathbf{p}$.\n3.  It shifts the UV coordinates, sampling the diffuse and normal maps at the offset location.\n\n$$\\Delta \\mathbf{p} = \\left( \\frac{V_x}{V_z}, \\frac{V_y}{V_z} \\right) \\cdot h \\cdot s$$\n\nWhere $s$ is a user-defined depth scale factor.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Parallax Occlusion Mapping (POM)\n\nBasic parallax mapping assumes the height map is flat within the offset step. For steep bumps, this assumption fails, causing textures to stretch or look distorted.\n\nTo render deep, complex details (like deep brick crevices or cobblestones), we use **Parallax Occlusion Mapping (POM)**. Instead of a single offset step, POM performs **Raymarching inside the fragment shader**:\n\n1.  It divides the height field into a series of horizontal layers.\n2.  It steps along the view ray vector in tangent space, sampling the height map at each layer.\n3.  It stops when the ray's depth is greater than or equal to the sampled height value.\n4.  It performs a linear interpolation between the last two sample points to find the exact intersection point.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Flat polygon plane -->\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <text x=\"60\" y=\"40\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Flat Triangle Surface (y=0)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Height Map curve boundary -->\n  <path d=\"M 50 160 Q 150 60 220 140 Q 300 190 350 90 Q 400 40 450 120\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Height Field Profile</text>\n  \n  <!-- View Ray in Tangent Space -->\n  <line x1=\"120\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"50\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">View Ray</text>\n  \n  <!-- Raymarch step points -->\n  <circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"69\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"88\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  \n  <!-- Intersection Point (Ray hits height curve) -->\n  <circle cx=\"242\" cy=\"108\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n  <text x=\"252\" y=\"112\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">Intersection point (UV Offset)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Performance Cost\n\nPOM provides high-quality displacement mapping but has a high performance cost:\n*   **Shader Complexity:** Raymarching requires loops and multiple texture fetches per pixel, which can bottleneck the GPU.\n*   **Optimization:** The shader dynamically scales the number of ray steps based on the view angle (shooting more steps at steep angles, and fewer when looking straight down).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (GLSL Fragment Shader)\n\nHere is a GLSL shader implementation of steep parallax mapping:\n\n```glsl\n#version 330 core\nout vec4 FragColor;\n\nin vec2 TexCoords;\nin vec3 TangentViewDir;\n\nuniform sampler2D diffuseMap;\nuniform sampler2D depthMap; // Height map (stored as depth values)\nuniform float heightScale;\n\nvec2 ParallaxMapping(vec2 texCoords, vec3 viewDir)\n{\n    // 1. Configure raymarch steps based on view angle\n    const float minLayers = 8.0;\n    const float maxLayers = 32.0;\n    float numLayers = mix(maxLayers, minLayers, abs(dot(vec3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0), viewDir)));\n    \n    // 2. Step size calculations\n    float layerDepth = 1.0 / numLayers;\n    float currentLayerDepth = 0.0;\n    \n    // Shift vector along view direction: P = (V.x, V.y) / V.z * scale\n    vec2 P = viewDir.xy / viewDir.z * heightScale;\n    vec2 deltaTexCoords = P / numLayers;\n    \n    vec2 currentTexCoords = texCoords;\n    float currentDepthMapValue = texture(depthMap, currentTexCoords).r;\n    \n    // 3. Raymarch loop\n    while(currentLayerDepth < currentDepthMapValue)\n    {\n        currentTexCoords -= deltaTexCoords;\n        currentDepthMapValue = texture(depthMap, currentTexCoords).r;\n        currentLayerDepth += layerDepth;\n    }\n    \n    return currentTexCoords; // Return the offset texture coordinate\n}\n\nvoid main()\n{\n    // Resolve parallax UV coordinates\n    vec3 V = normalize(TangentViewDir);\n    vec2 offsetCoords = ParallaxMapping(TexCoords, V);\n    \n    // Discard pixel if UV coordinates drift outside boundary\n    if(offsetCoords.x > 1.0 || offsetCoords.y > 1.0 || offsetCoords.x < 0.0 || offsetCoords.y < 0.0)\n        discard;\n        \n    vec3 color = texture(diffuseMap, offsetCoords).rgb;\n    FragColor = vec4(color, 1.0);\n}\n```\n",
    "title": "Parallax Mapping",
    "tagline": "Simulating depth displacement and self-occlusion using height map raymarching (Steep Parallax / POM).",
    "category": "Game Developer",
