    "slug": "game-gfx-color",
    "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Color Spaces & Linear Workflows\n\nColor representation in computer graphics is more than just choosing RGB values. To simulate lighting and materials accurately, we must understand the difference between **Linear Color Space** and **Gamma Color Space**. Failing to handle this correctly results in \"gamma errors\" (muddy lighting, incorrect gradients, and unrealistic shadows).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Human Visual Perception\n\nHuman eyes do not perceive light intensity linearly. We are much more sensitive to differences in dark shades than in bright ones. \n\nTo match this perception and maximize the representation efficiency of 8-bit images, monitors and cameras compress color values using a **Gamma curve** (typically sRGB standard, with an exponent of approximately $2.2$):\n\n$$V_{\\text{sRGB}} = V_{\\text{linear}}^{1/2.2}$$\n\nThis compression allocates more bits to shadows and fewer to bright highlights, matching human perception.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Grid Axes -->\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"440\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  \n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"25\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Stored Value (0.0 to 1.0)</text>\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"175\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Physical Brightness</text>\n  \n  <!-- Linear Ramp (Physics) -->\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"440\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Linear Ramp (Physics)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Gamma 2.2 Curve (Monitor/sRGB Encoding) -->\n  <path d=\"M 60 160 Q 200 40 440 40\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"3\" />\n  <text x=\"140\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">sRGB Gamma Curve (y = x^(1/2.2))</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Linear Workflow\n\nWhile sRGB is ideal for storage and display, performing physics calculations (such as adding light colors, multiplying textures, or interpolating vertex data) on sRGB values yields mathematically incorrect results. \n\nFor instance, in sRGB space:\n$$0.5 + 0.5 \\neq 1.0 \\quad \\text{(in terms of physical light energy)}$$\n\nTo simulate light correctly, engines use a **Linear Workflow**:\n\n```\n[sRGB Texture] ──> Decode (pow 2.2) ──> Linear Math Shaders (Add/Mul) ──> Encode (pow 1/2.2) ──> [Monitor]\n```\n\n1.  **Decode on Input:** When sampling a texture marked as sRGB, convert the colors to linear space in the shader:\n    $$V_{\\text{linear}} = V_{\\text{sRGB}}^{2.2}$$\n2.  **Shader Computations:** Perform all lighting, reflections, and post-processing calculations in Linear Space.\n3.  **Encode on Output:** Before writing the final color to the monitor's frame buffer, compress it back to sRGB space:\n    $$V_{\\text{display}} = V_{\\text{linear}}^{1/2.2}$$\n\nMost modern GPUs handle this conversion automatically in hardware if textures and frame buffers are configured with sRGB formats (e.g. `DXGI_FORMAT_R8G8B8A8_UNORM_SRGB`).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Shading Differences (Linear vs. Gamma)\n\nIf you render a scene without a linear workflow:\n*   **Shadow Transitions:** Gradients between light and shadow look harsh and dark, showing a steep \"black crush\" band.\n*   **Color Bleeding:** Blending red and green lights creates dark, muddy yellow-brown patches in the middle instead of bright yellow.\n*   **Light Falloff:** Lights appear to decay too quickly, requiring unrealistically high intensities to illuminate a room.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (GLSL Manual Conversion)\n\nIf hardware sRGB conversion is disabled, shaders perform the conversions manually:\n\n```glsl\n#version 330 core\nout vec4 FragColor;\n\nin vec2 TexCoords;\nuniform sampler2D diffuseTexture;\n\n// Decode sRGB color to Linear space\nvec3 sRGBToLinear(vec3 srgbColor)\n{\n    // Approximation: color^2.2\n    return pow(srgbColor, vec3(2.2));\n}\n\n// Encode Linear color back to sRGB space\nvec3 linearTosRGB(vec3 linearColor)\n{\n    // Approximation: color^(1/2.2)\n    return pow(linearColor, vec3(1.0 / 2.2));\n}\n\nvoid main()\n{\n    // 1. Sample sRGB texture and convert to Linear Space\n    vec3 rawColor = texture(diffuseTexture, TexCoords).rgb;\n    vec3 linearColor = sRGBToLinear(rawColor);\n    \n    // 2. Perform lighting calculations in Linear Space\n    vec3 lightColor = vec3(1.0, 0.9, 0.8); // Warm sun\n    float diffuseFactor = 0.8;\n    vec3 finalLinear = linearColor * lightColor * diffuseFactor;\n    \n    // 3. Compress final color to sRGB for monitor display\n    FragColor = vec4(linearTosRGB(finalLinear), 1.0);\n}\n```\n",
    "title": "Color Spaces & Linear Workflows",
    "tagline": "Human visual color sensitivity, sRGB gamma compression curves, and linear color space processing.",
