    "slug": "game-gfx-perception",
    "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Human Visual Perception\n\nComputer graphics aims to present images to the human eye. To design efficient rendering pipelines, color spaces, and compression formats, we must understand the biological and psychological characteristics of **Human Visual Perception**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Photoreceptor Cells: Rods and Cones\n\nThe retina contains two types of light-sensitive photoreceptor cells:\n\n*   **Cones:** Responsible for color vision and high-detail acuity. They operate in bright light (**Photopic Vision**). There are three types of cones, matching specific light wavelengths:\n    *   **S-Cones (Short):** Sensitive to Blue light ($\\sim 420$ nm).\n    *   **M-Cones (Medium):** Sensitive to Green light ($\\sim 534$ nm).\n    *   **L-Cones (Long):** Sensitive to Red light ($\\sim 564$ nm).\n*   **Rods:** Highly sensitive to low light levels (**Scotopic Vision**). They cannot perceive color and have low detail acuity but are highly sensitive to motion and peripheral shifts.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Grid Axes -->\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"440\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  \n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"25\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Cone Sensitivity</text>\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"195\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Wavelength (400nm to 700nm)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Blue Cone (S) -->\n  <path d=\"M 60 180 Q 110 50 160 180\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">S (Blue)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Green Cone (M) -->\n  <path d=\"M 120 180 Q 230 40 310 180\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"60\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">M (Green)</text>\n\n  <!-- Red Cone (L) -->\n  <path d=\"M 140 180 Q 260 30 360 180\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"280\" y=\"50\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">L (Red)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Relative Luminance Perception\n\nBecause our M and L cones overlap heavily in the yellow-green spectrum, the human eye is significantly more sensitive to **Green** and **Red** than to **Blue**.\n\nIn computer graphics, we calculate perceived brightness (**Relative Luminance**, defined by the ITU-R BT.709 standard) by applying different weights to the color channels:\n\n$$Y = 0.2126 R + 0.7152 G + 0.0722 B$$\n\n### Applications:\n*   **Compression (YUV/YCbCr):** Since we are more sensitive to brightness ($Y$) than to color (chrominance $U, V$), video and image formats (like JPEG) compress color data at a higher ratio than brightness details (chroma subsampling).\n*   **Luminance Edge Detection:** Post-processing shaders (like FXAA or bloom) use the relative luminance formula to locate high-contrast edges and highlights.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Dynamic Range & Eye Adaptation\n\nThe human eye can perceive light levels spanning across 10 orders of magnitude (from dim starlight to bright sunlight). We achieve this through **Adaptation**: adjusting the pupil size and chemical sensitivity of photoreceptors over time.\n\nWhen a player walks from a bright sunny field into a dark cave:\n*   The virtual camera simulates this transition by gradually increasing the exposure threshold over several seconds, slowly revealing details inside the dark room.\n*   Moving from dark to bright (e.g. exiting the cave) triggers a rapid \"blinding flash\" effect, followed by a quick adaptation decay.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Axes -->\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"440\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  \n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"30\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Virtual Exposure (Gain)</text>\n  <text x=\"380\" y=\"175\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Time (s)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Bright to Dark Adaptation Curve (Slow adaptation) -->\n  <path d=\"M 60 140 Q 200 120 440 50\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" />\n  <text x=\"140\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Bright -> Dark (Slow adaptation, ~5-10s)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Dark to Bright Adaptation Curve (Rapid adaptation) -->\n  <path d=\"M 60 50 L 120 140 Q 280 140 440 140\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" />\n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"115\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Dark -> Bright (Rapid, ~1-2s)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (GLSL Relative Luminance Shader)\n\n```glsl\n#version 330 core\nout vec4 FragColor;\n\nin vec2 TexCoords;\nuniform sampler2D screenTexture;\n\nfloat calculateLuminance(vec3 color)\n{\n    // ITU-R BT.709 relative luminance coefficients\n    return dot(color, vec3(0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722));\n}\n\nvoid main()\n{\n    vec3 sceneColor = texture(screenTexture, TexCoords).rgb;\n    float lum = calculateLuminance(sceneColor);\n    \n    // Output grayscale luminance map (useful for Bloom/Post-process mask)\n    FragColor = vec4(vec3(lum), 1.0);\n}\n```\n",
    "title": "Human Visual Perception",
    "tagline": "Cones/rods sensitivity limits, BT.709 relative luminance weighting, and eye adaptation curves.",
