    "slug": "game-gfx-tone",
    "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Tone Mapping Operators\n\nIn modern 3D games, lighting and specular highlights are calculated using physical light intensities that can exceed the display range of standard monitors by thousands of times. This range of lighting is called **High Dynamic Range (HDR)**. \n\nTo display these images on standard **Low Dynamic Range (LDR)** monitors (which can only display values from $[0.0, 1.0]$), we use **Tone Mapping** to compress color values while preserving highlights and detail.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Tone Mapping Operators\n\nA tone mapping operator is a mathematical function that maps HDR values $[0.0, \\infty)$ to LDR values $[0.0, 1.0]$. The shape of this curve determines the contrast, shadow details, and highlight rolls in the final image.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Grid Axes -->\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"440\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  \n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"25\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Output LDR (0.0 to 1.0)</text>\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"195\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Input HDR (0.0 to 5.0+)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Linear Clamp (Clips at 1.0) -->\n  <!-- Starts at (60,180), goes to (140, 40), then flat to (440, 40) -->\n  <path d=\"M 60 180 L 140 40 L 440 40\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n  <text x=\"130\" y=\"55\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Linear Clamp (Clips/Burns out)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Reinhard Curve (Simple asymptotic decay) -->\n  <!-- y = x / (1 + x) -->\n  <path d=\"M 60 180 Q 200 80 440 55\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Reinhard (Smooth, low contrast)</text>\n\n  <!-- ACES Filmic S-Curve -->\n  <path d=\"M 60 180 C 130 180 180 40 440 40\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\" />\n  <text x=\"230\" y=\"65\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">ACES Filmic (Toe &amp; Shoulder)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Common Mathematical Formulations\n\n### 2.1 Reinhard Tone Mapping (Global Operator)\nThe simplest operator, mapping values smoothly using an asymptotic curve:\n\n$$T(x) = \\frac{x}{1 + x}$$\n\n*   **Pros:** Easy to compute, guarantees no pixel burns out past $1.0$.\n*   **Cons:** Tends to wash out highly saturated colors and compresses mid-tones too aggressively, reducing image contrast.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 ACES Tone Mapping (Filmic Curve)\nThe **Academy Color Encoding System (ACES)** tone mapper is the industry standard for modern game engines. It uses an S-shaped curve that mimics the physical behavior of chemical film:\n\n$$T(x) = \\frac{x(ax + b)}{x(cx + d) + e}$$\n\nWhere:\n*   $a = 2.51, \\quad b = 0.03, \\quad c = 2.43, \\quad d = 0.59, \\quad e = 0.14$.\n\n#### Features of the S-Curve:\n1.  **The Toe:** A flat region at the bottom of the curve that increases contrast in shadows, keeping blacks deep and rich.\n2.  **The Linear Section:** The middle section that maintains a linear exposure for natural mid-tones.\n3.  **The Shoulder:** The flat region at the top that smoothly rolls off bright highlights, preserving detail in bright areas (like glowing suns or explosions).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Implementation Example (GLSL Fragment Shader)\n\nHere is a post-processing fragment shader demonstrating Reinhard and ACES tone mapping:\n\n```glsl\n#version 330 core\nout vec4 FragColor;\n\nin vec2 TexCoords;\nuniform sampler2D hdrBuffer;\nuniform int toneMapType; // 0 = Reinhard, 1 = ACES\n\n// Reinhard global operator\nvec3 ReinhardToneMapping(vec3 color)\n{\n    return color / (color + vec3(1.0));\n}\n\n// ACES Filmic fitted curve\nvec3 ACESToneMapping(vec3 x)\n{\n    float a = 2.51;\n    float b = 0.03;\n    float c = 2.43;\n    float d = 0.59;\n    float e = 0.14;\n    return clamp((x * (a * x + b)) / (x * (c * x + d) + e), 0.0, 1.0);\n}\n\nvoid main()\n{\n    vec3 hdrColor = texture(hdrBuffer, TexCoords).rgb;\n    vec3 mappedColor = vec3(0.0);\n    \n    if (toneMapType == 0)\n    {\n        mappedColor = ReinhardToneMapping(hdrColor);\n    }\n    else\n    {\n        mappedColor = ACESToneMapping(hdrColor);\n    }\n    \n    // Output final color (monitor gamma correction is done afterward)\n    FragColor = vec4(mappedColor, 1.0);\n}\n```\n",
    "title": "Tone Mapping Operators",
    "tagline": "Compressing dynamic range values using Reinhard global operators and ACES filmic S-curves.",
    "category": "Game Developer",
    "readTime": 15,
