    "slug": "game-light-cascaded",
    "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Cascaded Shadow Maps (CSM)\n\n**Cascaded Shadow Maps (CSM)**, also known as Parallel Split Shadow Maps (PSSM), is a rendering technique used to resolve **Perspective Aliasing** in outdoor scenes illuminated by directional lights.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Perspective Aliasing Problem\n\nDirectional lights (like the sun) illuminate the entire scene. If we use a single shadow map to cover the entire viewable area:\n*   **Close to the Camera:** A single pixel on the screen maps to a tiny fraction of a shadow map texel. This results in highly pixelated, jagged shadow edges.\n*   **Far from the Camera:** The shadow resolution is sufficient, but we waste valuable texture coordinates rendering shadows on objects that are too far away to see in detail.\n\nThis resolution mismatch is called **Perspective Aliasing**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The CSM Solution\n\nInstead of rendering a single shadow map, CSM splits the camera's view frustum into multiple depth ranges called **Cascades** (typically 3 or 4 splits):\n\n*   **Cascade 0 (Near):** Covers the region closest to the player. The orthographic bounding box is small, providing high-resolution shadows.\n*   **Cascade 1 (Medium):** Covers the mid-ground. Bounding box is larger; shadow resolution is moderate.\n*   **Cascade 2 (Far):** Covers background mountains and buildings. Bounding box is massive; shadow resolution is low.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Camera/Eye -->\n  <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"110\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"35\" y=\"135\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Camera</text>\n  \n  <!-- Frustum lines -->\n  <line x1=\"56\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"56\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  \n  <!-- Split 1: Cascade 0 (Near) -->\n  <path d=\"M 56 110 L 150 90 V 130 Z\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"90\" y=\"115\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"10\">Cascade 0</text>\n  \n  <!-- Split 2: Cascade 1 (Medium) -->\n  <path d=\"M 150 90 L 270 70 V 150 L 150 130 Z\" fill=\"#10b981\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"115\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"10\">Cascade 1</text>\n  \n  <!-- Split 3: Cascade 2 (Far) -->\n  <path d=\"M 270 70 L 420 50 V 170 L 270 150 Z\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"115\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"10\">Cascade 2</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Frustum Splitting: Spacing Calculations\n\nTo calculate the depth boundaries ($z_i$) for each split, engines combine **Uniform Spacing** (linear split steps) and **Logarithmic Spacing** (steps that scale exponentially with depth). This combination is called the **Practical Split Scheme**:\n\n$$z_i = \\lambda z_{\\text{near}} \\left( \\frac{z_{\\text{far}}}{z_{\\text{near}}} \\right)^{i/N} + (1 - \\lambda) \\left( z_{\\text{near}} + \\frac{i}{N} (z_{\\text{far}} - z_{\\text{near}}) \\right)$$\n\nWhere:\n*   $N$ is the total number of cascades.\n*   $i$ is the index of the current split ($1 \\le i \\le N$).\n*   $\\lambda$ is a blending weight factor (typically set to $\\sim 0.85$ to prioritize logarithmic spacing, keeping splits close to the camera).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python - Cascade Splitting Bounds)\n\n```python\nimport math\n\nclass CascadeSplitter:\n    def __init__(self, near, far, num_cascades=3, lambda_weight=0.85):\n        self.near = near\n        self.far = far\n        self.num_cascades = num_cascades\n        self.lambda_weight = lambda_weight\n        \n    def calculate_splits(self):\n        splits = [self.near]\n        \n        for i in range(1, self.num_cascades + 1):\n            ratio = i / self.num_cascades\n            \n            # Logarithmic Split\n            log_split = self.near * math.pow(self.far / self.near, ratio)\n            \n            # Uniform Split\n            uniform_split = self.near + ratio * (self.far - self.near)\n            \n            # Blended Split: Practical split scheme formula\n            split_depth = self.lambda_weight * log_split + (1.0 - self.lambda_weight) * uniform_split\n            splits.append(split_depth)\n            \n        return splits # List of Z boundaries (e.g. [0.1, 10.0, 45.0, 150.0])\n```\n",
    "title": "Cascaded Shadow Maps (CSM)",
    "tagline": "Resolving perspective aliasing in outdoor scenes using blended split schemes.",
    "category": "Game Developer",
