    "slug": "game-light-infinite",
    "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Infinite / Directional Light Bounds\n\nBecause directional lights (like the Sun) shine across the entire virtual world, their projection boundaries are theoretically infinite. To render high-quality shadows in real-time, we must restrict the light's orthographic projection boundaries to enclose only the **Camera's View Frustum**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Bounding Box Fitting Algorithm\n\nTo calculate the tightest orthographic projection matrix for a directional light:\n\n1.  **Extract Frustum Corners:** Find the 8 corner vertices of the camera's view frustum in world space (4 corners on the near plane, 4 on the far plane).\n2.  **Transform to Light Space:** Transform these 8 vertices into the light source's view space using the light's view matrix $M_{\\text{light\\_view}}$ (where the Z-axis aligns with the light's direction).\n3.  **Calculate Extents:** Find the minimum and maximum coordinates ($x_{\\text{min}}, x_{\\text{max}}, y_{\\text{min}}, y_{\\text{max}}, z_{\\text{min}}, z_{\\text{max}}$) among the 8 light-space vertices.\n4.  **Construct Orthographic Projection:** Use these bounds to build the orthographic projection matrix:\n    $$M_{\\text{ortho}} = \\text{Ortho}(x_{\\text{min}}, x_{\\text{max}}, y_{\\text{min}}, y_{\\text{max}}, z_{\\text{min}}, z_{\\text{max}})$$\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Camera Frustum (Transformed to Light View Space) -->\n  <polygon points=\"120,80 200,60 300,140 180,165\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"80\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"60\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"140\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"165\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  \n  <text x=\"140\" y=\"115\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Camera Frustum</text>\n  \n  <!-- Fitted Orthographic Bounding Box -->\n  <!-- Bounds: xmin = 120, xmax = 300, ymin = 60, ymax = 165 -->\n  <rect x=\"120\" y=\"60\" width=\"180\" height=\"105\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n  <text x=\"125\" y=\"50\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">Tight Fitting Orthographic Bounding Box</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Temporal Stability & Texel Snapping\n\nIf you recreate the projection matrix in every frame using the algorithm above, you will notice that the shadow borders appear to crawl and flicker whenever the camera moves or rotates. \n\nThis **shadow shimmering** occurs because the boundaries shift by fractional pixels, causing the shadow map texels to land on different world coordinates in each frame.\n\n### Solution: Texel Snapping\nTo achieve temporal stability, we snap the orthographic boundary coordinates ($x_{\\text{min}}, y_{\\text{min}}$) to increments of the shadow map's texel size in world units:\n\n$$\\text{texelSize} = \\frac{x_{\\text{max}} - x_{\\text{min}}}{\\text{shadowMapWidth}}$$\n$$x_{\\text{min\\_snapped}} = \\text{floor}\\left( \\frac{x_{\\text{min}}}{\\text{texelSize}} \\right) \\cdot \\text{texelSize}$$\n\nSnapping prevents shadow coordinates from shifting by sub-pixel offsets, eliminating the shimmering artifact.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Implementation Example (Python - Texel Snapping Bounds)\n\n```python\nimport math\n\nclass OrthoBoundsSnapper:\n    def __init__(self, shadow_map_resolution):\n        self.resolution = float(shadow_map_resolution)\n        \n    def snap_bounds(self, x_min, x_max, y_min, y_max):\n        # 1. Calculate bounding box dimensions\n        x_range = x_max - x_min\n        y_range = y_max - y_min\n        \n        # 2. Calculate the size of a single shadow map texel in world space\n        texel_size_x = x_range / self.resolution\n        texel_size_y = y_range / self.resolution\n        \n        # 3. Snap min bounds to integer multiples of texel size\n        x_min_snapped = math.floor(x_min / texel_size_x) * texel_size_x\n        y_min_snapped = math.floor(y_min / texel_size_y) * texel_size_y\n        \n        # 4. Re-calculate max bounds to keep size exactly consistent\n        x_max_snapped = x_min_snapped + x_range\n        y_max_snapped = y_min_snapped + y_range\n        \n        return x_min_snapped, x_max_snapped, y_min_snapped, y_max_snapped\n```\n",
    "title": "Infinite / Directional Light Bounds",
    "tagline": "Fitting directional light orthographic projections to view frustums with texel snapping.",
