    "slug": "game-vis-occluder",
    "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Occluders & Occlusion Culling\n\nEven after discarding objects outside the camera's field of view (frustum culling), we often waste GPU cycles rendering objects that are completely hidden behind closer, solid obstacles (such as walls, buildings, or mountains). \n\nTo prevent this waste, we use **Occlusion Culling** to identify and discard hidden objects.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Occluders vs. Occludees\n\n*   **Occluders:** Large, solid geometries (like concrete walls or terrain mountains) that block the camera's view of objects behind them.\n*   **Occludees:** Objects situated behind occluders (like characters, furniture, or vegetation) that should be culled.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Camera -->\n  <circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"110\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"45\" y=\"135\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Camera</text>\n  \n  <!-- View rays -->\n  <line x1=\"66\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <line x1=\"66\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  \n  <!-- Solid Occluder (Wall) -->\n  <rect x=\"200\" y=\"60\" width=\"40\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" />\n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"50\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Occluder (Wall)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Occluded Object -->\n  <polygon points=\"340,90 380,70 400,110 360,130\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"155\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Occludee (Hidden Object)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Culling Tag -->\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"25\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">CULLED (0 pixels visible)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Hardware Occlusion Queries\n\nModern GPUs provide built-in queries to assist with occlusion culling:\n\n1.  **Render Occluders First:** Render all large, known occluders to populate the depth buffer.\n2.  **Issue Query:** Begin an GPU occlusion query block.\n3.  **Render Bounding Boxes:** Disable writing to the color and depth buffers, and render the simple bounding box of an occludee.\n4.  **End Query & Check:** The GPU counts how many pixels of the bounding box passed the depth test.\n    *   **Count = 0:** The bounding box is completely hidden behind the occluders. The engine **culls** the object, skipping its complex high-polygon mesh draw calls.\n    *   **Count > 0:** Part of the bounding box is visible. Render the full mesh normally.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hierarchical Z-Buffer (HZB) Oculling\n\nHardware queries have a latency penalty: the CPU must wait for the GPU to return the pixel count result before deciding whether to draw the mesh, which can stall the rendering pipeline.\n\nTo bypass this latency, modern engines use **Hierarchical Z-Buffer (HZB) Culling**:\n1.  Downsample the depth buffer (Z-buffer) into a mipmap pyramid using **max-pooling** (storing the furthest depth in each $2\\times2$ grid cell).\n2.  A compute shader projects the bounding box of each object into screen space and determines the correct HZB mipmap level.\n3.  It compares the object's closest depth against the maximum depth stored in the HZB cell. If the object is further than the HZB depth, it is culled instantly on the GPU.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python - Bounding Box Raster Occlusion Check)\n\n```python\nclass BoundingBox2D:\n    def __init__(self, x_min, x_max, y_min, y_max, z_min):\n        self.xmin = x_min\n        self.xmax = x_max\n        self.ymin = y_min\n        self.ymax = y_max\n        self.zmin = z_min # Closest depth of the bounding box\n\ndef is_occluded_hzb(box, hzb_mipmap):\n    # Sample the HZB mipmap corresponding to the bounding box screen dimensions\n    # Let's assume we map box.xmin, ymin directly to the HZB pixel grid coordinates\n    grid_x0 = int(box.xmin)\n    grid_x1 = int(box.xmax)\n    grid_y0 = int(box.ymin)\n    grid_y1 = int(box.ymax)\n    \n    max_depth_in_region = 0.0\n    \n    # Check the maximum depth in the HZB buffer over the box's coverage area\n    for y in range(grid_y0, grid_y1 + 1):\n        for x in range(grid_x0, grid_x1 + 1):\n            depth_val = hzb_mipmap.get_pixel(x, y) # Stores the furthest depth value\n            max_depth_in_region = max(max_depth_in_region, depth_val)\n            \n    # If the closest point of the bounding box is further than the furthest depth \n    # value of the occluders in that region, the entire box is occluded.\n    if box.zmin > max_depth_in_region:\n        return True # Culled\n        \n    return False # Visible\n```\n",
    "title": "Occluders & Occlusion Culling",
    "tagline": "Discarding blocked meshes using hardware occlusion queries and Hierarchical Z-Buffers (HZB).",
    "category": "Game Developer",
