    "slug": "game-vis-light",
    "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Light Volume Culling & Tiled Shading\n\nIn games with hundreds of dynamic lights (like cities at night or dungeons with fire particles), computing lighting equations for every object against every light source scales at $O(\\text{Objects} \\times \\text{Lights})$, quickly bottlenecking the GPU. \n\nTo solve this, engines use **Light Volume Culling** and advanced shading architectures (like **Forward+** and **Clustered Shading**).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Forward vs. Deferred Shading\n\n*   **Forward Rendering:** Every mesh is drawn, and its pixel colors are computed against active light sources in a single pass.\n    *   *Problem:* If multiple meshes overlap (Overdraw), we waste GPU cycles calculating lights for pixels that are subsequently drawn over.\n*   **Deferred Shading:** \n    1.  Render all scene geometries to intermediate textures storing material properties (Color, Normal, Position, Specular) called the **G-Buffer**.\n    2.  For each light source, render its 3D **Light Volume** (e.g. bounding sphere for point lights, cone for spotlights) as a geometry pass.\n    3.  The lighting equations are evaluated only for G-buffer pixels overlapping the light volume.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tiled Shading (Forward+)\n\nWhile deferred shading handles millions of lights, it cannot easily render transparent objects or support multiple custom material shading models. To combine the flexibility of Forward rendering with the light scalability of Deferred rendering, engines use **Tiled Forward Shading (Forward+)**:\n\n1.  **Screen Slicing:** Slice the screen into a 2D grid of small tiles (typically $16 \\times 16$ pixels).\n2.  **Calculate Depth Limits:** Find the minimum and maximum depth ($z$) values of the geometry within each tile.\n3.  **Light Culling:** For each tile, construct a 3D bounding box using the tile's screen boundaries and its depth limits. Test which light volumes intersect this box.\n4.  **Light List Generation:** Build a list of active light indices for each tile.\n5.  **Shading Pass:** In the forward shading pass, each fragment looks up its screen tile coordinates and loops **only** over the light sources listed for that tile.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Screen grid tiles -->\n  <g stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n    <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"200\" />\n    <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"200\" />\n    <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"200\" />\n    <line x1=\"400\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"200\" />\n    \n    <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"80\" />\n    <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"140\" />\n  </g>\n  \n  <text x=\"60\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">2D Tile Grid</text>\n  \n  <!-- Light Volume 1 (Overlapping specific tiles) -->\n  <circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"110\" r=\"45\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"110\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <text x=\"140\" y=\"95\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Light A</text>\n  \n  <!-- Light Volume 2 -->\n  <circle cx=\"340\" cy=\"110\" r=\"55\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <circle cx=\"340\" cy=\"110\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"95\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Light B</text>\n  \n  <!-- Highlighted Tile (100-200, 80-140) with Light A index -->\n  <rect x=\"100\" y=\"80\" width=\"100\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#10b981\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"9\">Tile Light List: [A]</text>\n  \n  <!-- Overlap Tile (200-300, 80-140) with Light A and B indexes -->\n  <rect x=\"200\" y=\"80\" width=\"100\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#a855f7\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#a855f7\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"205\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#a855f7\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"9\">Tile Light List: [A, B]</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Clustered Shading\n\nTiled shading fails when a tile has a large depth range (e.g. a long hallway where a player stands close to a wall, but a window shows a mountain far away). The depth range is huge, so the tile's bounding box encompasses the entire hallway, listing lights that are actually far away from the local geometry.\n\n**Clustered Shading** solves this by slicing the view frustum not just in 2D tiles, but also along the Z depth axis, dividing the view frustum volume into a 3D grid of **voxel clusters**. The engine assigns lights to these 3D clusters, providing extremely tight light filtering.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python - Light Tile Intersection test)\n\n```python\nclass LightVolumeSphere:\n    def __init__(self, x, y, z, r):\n        self.center = (x, y, z)\n        self.radius = r\n\nclass AABB3D:\n    def __init__(self, x_min, x_max, y_min, y_max, z_min, z_max):\n        self.xmin = x_min\n        self.xmax = x_max\n        self.ymin = y_min\n        self.ymax = y_max\n        self.zmin = z_min\n        self.zmax = z_max\n\ndef intersects_light_tile(light, tile_box):\n    # Test intersection between point light bounding sphere and 3D tile bounding box\n    # Find closest coordinate point on AABB to sphere center\n    cx = max(tile_box.xmin, min(light.center[0], tile_box.xmax))\n    cy = max(tile_box.ymin, min(light.center[1], tile_box.ymax))\n    cz = max(tile_box.zmin, min(light.center[2], tile_box.zmax))\n    \n    # Calculate distance from closest point to sphere center\n    dist_sq = (cx - light.center[0])**2 + (cy - light.center[1])**2 + (cz - light.center[2])**2\n    \n    # If distance squared is less than radius squared, intersection occurs\n    return dist_sq <= (light.radius * light.radius)\n```\n",
    "title": "Light Volume Culling & Tiled Shading",
    "tagline": "Scale limits optimizations: G-Buffer, screen-space tiled lists, and 3D cluster voxels.",
