    "slug": "game-api-opengl",
    "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: OpenGL API & State Machine Bindings\n\n**OpenGL (Open Graphics Library)** is a cross-language, cross-platform API for rendering 2D and 3D vector graphics. Unlike modern APIs (like Vulkan or D3D12), OpenGL is built on a **Global State Machine** design model.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Global State Machine Model\n\nOpenGL operates as a giant state machine. Instead of passing parameters directly to draw calls, you modify global states inside an active **OpenGL Context**:\n\n```cpp\nglBindTexture(GL_TEXTURE_2D, myTexture); // Global state: \"Active 2D Texture Slot\" is now myTexture\nglUseProgram(myShaderProgram);           // Global state: \"Active Shader Program\" is now myShaderProgram\nglDrawArrays(GL_TRIANGLES, 0, 3);        // Draw call reads active texture and shader slots automatically\n```\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 240\" width=\"100%\" height=\"240\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- OpenGL Context Frame -->\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"30\" width=\"440\" height=\"180\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"45\" y=\"25\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">Active OpenGL Context (State Machine)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Global State Slots -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(50, 50)\">\n    <!-- Slot 1: Program -->\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"10\" width=\"180\" height=\"35\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1\" rx=\"2\" />\n    <text x=\"10\" y=\"32\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Active Shader: Shader_0x4F</text>\n    \n    <!-- Slot 2: Texture -->\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"60\" width=\"180\" height=\"35\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1\" rx=\"2\" />\n    <text x=\"10\" y=\"82\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Active Texture: Texture_0x9A</text>\n\n    <!-- Slot 3: VAO -->\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"110\" width=\"180\" height=\"35\" fill=\"#10b981\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1\" rx=\"2\" />\n    <text x=\"10\" y=\"132\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Active VAO: VAO_0x12</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  <!-- Overwrite command illustration -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(260, 50)\">\n    <rect x=\"30\" y=\"60\" width=\"160\" height=\"35\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"3\" />\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"82\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">glBindTexture(Tex_New)</text>\n    <!-- Arrow pointing to state overwrite -->\n    <path d=\"M 25 77 L -20 77\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-amber)\" />\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n### Limitations of the State Machine:\n*   **Threading bottlenecks:** Only one CPU thread can have the OpenGL Context bound as \"active\" at a time. This makes it impossible to record draw calls in parallel across multiple worker threads.\n*   **Driver Overhead:** Because the driver must validate the global state before every draw call (e.g. checking if the active shader matches the active buffer attributes), drivers have significant overhead, reducing performance compared to Vulkan.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Core Objects: VAO, VBO, and EBO\n\nTo render geometry efficiently, OpenGL groups vertex parameters into GPU-side objects:\n\n1.  **Vertex Buffer Object (VBO):** A buffer storing raw vertex data (positions, colors, normals) directly on GPU memory.\n2.  **Element Buffer Object (EBO):** A buffer storing vertex indices to enable indexed rendering (reusing vertex data to draw adjacent triangles).\n3.  **Vertex Array Object (VAO):** A wrapper object that stores VBO configuration bindings. By binding a single VAO, OpenGL restores all attribute pointer configurations instantly:\n\n```cpp\n// 1. Generate and bind VAO\nglGenVertexArrays(1, &vao);\nglBindVertexArray(vao);\n\n// 2. Bind and configure VBO\nglGenBuffers(1, &vbo);\nglBindBuffer(GL_ARRAY_BUFFER, vbo);\nglBufferData(GL_ARRAY_BUFFER, sizeof(vertices), vertices, GL_STATIC_DRAW);\n\n// 3. Configure Vertex Attribute pointers (link VBO to Shader input variables)\nglVertexAttribPointer(0, 3, GL_FLOAT, GL_FALSE, 3 * sizeof(float), (void*)0);\nglEnableVertexAttribArray(0);\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Implementation Example (C++ OpenGL Rendering Loop)\n\nThis implementation structure outlines a standard OpenGL draw loop:\n\n```cpp\n#include <GL/glew.h>\n#include <GLFW/glfw3.h>\n\nvoid RenderScene(unsigned int vao, unsigned int shaderProgram, int indexCount)\n{\n    // 1. Clear color and depth buffers\n    glClearColor(0.1f, 0.1f, 0.15f, 1.0f);\n    glClear(GL_COLOR_BUFFER_BIT | GL_DEPTH_BUFFER_BIT);\n    \n    // 2. Bind Shader Program\n    glUseProgram(shaderProgram);\n    \n    // 3. Update global Uniform variables (e.g. projection matrix)\n    int projLocation = glGetUniformLocation(shaderProgram, \"projection\");\n    // glUniformMatrix4fv(projLocation, 1, GL_FALSE, &projectionMatrix[0][0]);\n    \n    // 4. Bind Vertex Array (restores all buffer bindings automatically)\n    glBindVertexArray(vao);\n    \n    // 5. Issue Indexed Draw Call\n    glDrawElements(GL_TRIANGLES, indexCount, GL_UNSIGNED_INT, 0);\n    \n    // 6. Unbind objects to avoid accidental state corruption\n    glBindVertexArray(0);\n    glUseProgram(0);\n}\n```\n",
