    "slug": "game-api-opengles",
    "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: OpenGL ES & Mobile GPU Architectures\n\n**OpenGL ES (OpenGL for Embedded Systems)** is a lightweight subset of the OpenGL API designed for mobile, console, and embedded devices (including iOS, Android, and the Nintendo Switch). \n\nTo write high-performance mobile games, developers must understand not only the API but also the unique physical constraints of mobile hardware, specifically **Tile-Based Rendering**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Evolution of the OpenGL ES Specification\n\n*   **OpenGL ES 1.x:** Fixed-Function pipeline. No custom shaders allowed; lighting and transformations were configured using fixed API toggles.\n*   **OpenGL ES 2.0:** Programmable Pipeline. Introduced vertex and fragment shaders written in GLSL ES, matching the desktop OpenGL 2.0 architecture.\n*   **OpenGL ES 3.0/3.1/3.2:** Modern features. Added support for Multiple Render Targets (MRT), Instanced Rendering, Transform Feedback, Compute Shaders, and ASTC texture compression.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Mobile GPU Architectures: TBDR\n\nDesktop GPUs are typically **Immediate Mode Renderers (IMR)**: they draw every triangle immediately to the screen buffer. This requires constant read/write access to the main system memory bus, which consumes a significant amount of power and generates heat—both of which are highly restricted on battery-powered mobile devices.\n\nTo minimize memory bandwidth, mobile GPUs (such as Apple Silicon, ARM Mali, and PowerVR) use **TBDR (Tile-Based Deferred Rendering)**:\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 240\" width=\"100%\" height=\"240\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Triangles input -->\n  <polygon points=\"30,80 80,40 60,110\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"25\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Geometry</text>\n  \n  <line x1=\"90\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-zinc)\" />\n  \n  <!-- Step 1: Binning -->\n  <rect x=\"130\" y=\"45\" width=\"100\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"3\" />\n  <text x=\"145\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"10\">1. Tile Binning</text>\n  <text x=\"140\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"8\">(Sorts by screen tile)</text>\n  \n  <line x1=\"240\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"270\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-zinc)\" />\n\n  <!-- Step 2: Local cache shading -->\n  <rect x=\"280\" y=\"45\" width=\"100\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#10b981\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"3\" />\n  <text x=\"290\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"10\">2. Tile Shading</text>\n  <text x=\"290\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"8\">(Local GPU SRAM)</text>\n  \n  <line x1=\"390\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-zinc)\" />\n  \n  <!-- Step 3: Write out to RAM -->\n  <rect x=\"430\" y=\"55\" width=\"50\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#a855f7\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#a855f7\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"3\" />\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#a855f7\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">RAM</text>\n  \n  <!-- Explanatory note -->\n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"195\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\">TBDR: Only writes final resolved pixel colors to main RAM (Saves battery)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n### How TBDR Works:\n1.  **Binning Pass:** The GPU collects all triangles for a frame and sorts them into screen-space tiles (e.g. $16 \\times 16$ or $32 \\times 32$ pixel blocks).\n2.  **Shading Pass:** The GPU processes one tile at a time. It loads the geometry for a tile into a high-speed, local **On-Chip SRAM Cache**.\n3.  **Hidden Surface Removal:** Because the entire tile's geometry is local, the GPU performs depth checks and discards hidden pixels **before** running the pixel shader (saving shading calculations).\n4.  **Write Out:** The completed tile color is written out to main system memory.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Optimization Rules for Mobile Games\n\nTo keep mobile games running smoothly:\n*   **Avoid Render Target Changes:** Changing framebuffers forces the GPU to dump the local tile cache back to main system memory, defeating the benefits of TBDR. Keep render-to-texture switches to a minimum.\n*   **Discard Unused Buffers:** Explicitly tell the API when depth or stencil buffers are no longer needed (using `glDiscardFramebufferEXT` or `glInvalidateFramebuffer`), preventing the GPU from wasting bandwidth writing these buffers to RAM.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (C++ OpenGL ES Framebuffer Discard)\n\n```cpp\n#include <GLES3/gl3.h>\n\nvoid PerformMobilePostProcess(unsigned int postProcessFBO)\n{\n    // Bind target post-process framebuffer\n    glBindFramebuffer(GL_FRAMEBUFFER, postProcessFBO);\n    \n    // ... Draw post-process quad ...\n    \n    // OPTIMIZATION: Tell the mobile GPU driver to discard depth and stencil buffers.\n    // This prevents the driver from copying these temporary buffers back to the system RAM.\n    GLenum attachments[] = { GL_DEPTH_ATTACHMENT, GL_STENCIL_ATTACHMENT };\n    glInvalidateFramebuffer(GL_FRAMEBUFFER, 2, attachments);\n    \n    // Unbind\n    glBindFramebuffer(GL_FRAMEBUFFER, 0);\n}\n```\n",
