    "slug": "game-api-webgl",
    "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: WebGL Browser API\n\n**WebGL (Web Graphics Library)** is a JavaScript API designed for rendering interactive 3D and 2D graphics inside any compatible web browser without the use of plug-ins. It maps JavaScript bindings directly to the underlying hardware graphics driver.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WebGL 1.0 vs. WebGL 2.0\n\n*   **WebGL 1.0:** Based on the mobile **OpenGL ES 2.0** specification. Uses GLSL ES 1.0. Lacks native support for uniform buffer objects, multiple render targets, and instanced draw calls.\n*   **WebGL 2.0:** Based on **OpenGL ES 3.0**. Uses GLSL ES 3.0. Adds support for Instanced Arrays (crucial for particle systems/forests), Transform Feedback, 3D Textures, and integer vertex attributes.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The JavaScript-to-GPU Boundary\n\nBecause JavaScript is an interpreted, garbage-collected language, communicating with raw GPU memory has performance bottlenecks:\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- JS Engine (Garbage collected) -->\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"50\" width=\"120\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"3\" />\n  <text x=\"35\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"10\">JS Engine</text>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"95\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"8\">(GC Overhead / Slow)</text>\n  \n  <line x1=\"145\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"175\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-zinc)\" />\n  \n  <!-- Typed Array (Continuous Binary memory) -->\n  <rect x=\"180\" y=\"50\" width=\"130\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#10b981\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"3\" />\n  <text x=\"195\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"10\">Float32Array</text>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"95\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"8\">(Contiguous Binary)</text>\n  \n  <line x1=\"315\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"345\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-zinc)\" />\n  \n  <!-- WebGL Context API -->\n  <rect x=\"350\" y=\"50\" width=\"130\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"3\" />\n  <text x=\"370\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"10\">WebGL Context</text>\n  <text x=\"365\" y=\"95\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"8\">Browser Compositor</text>\n  \n  <!-- Direct path down to GPU -->\n  <path d=\"M 415 115 V 170 H 220\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#a855f7\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-purple)\" />\n  <rect x=\"80\" y=\"150\" width=\"130\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#a855f7\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#a855f7\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"3\" />\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"175\" fill=\"#a855f7\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">GPU VRAM Buffer</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n1.  **Strict Typed Arrays:** Standard JavaScript arrays (`[]`) are dynamic lists, which are too slow for shader pipelines. WebGL requires allocating data in contiguous binary memory buffers using typed arrays: `Float32Array` or `Int32Array`.\n2.  **Draw Call Batching:** JavaScript-to-WebGL API state switches incur a significant engine translation penalty. To maintain 60 FPS, engines must combine objects into large, single instanced draw calls rather than calling individual `gl.drawArrays` operations for separate meshes.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Implementation Example: WebGL 2.0 Initialization and Render\n\nThis complete JavaScript file demonstrates how to bind buffers and draw a colored triangle in WebGL 2:\n\n```javascript\n// 1. Get WebGL 2 Context from HTML Canvas element\nconst canvas = document.getElementById(\"glcanvas\");\nconst gl = canvas.getContext(\"webgl2\");\n\nif (!gl) {\n    console.error(\"WebGL 2.0 not supported in this browser!\");\n}\n\n// 2. Define vertices using a contiguous Float32Array binary buffer\nconst vertices = new Float32Array([\n    -0.5, -0.5, 0.0,  // Vertex 1\n     0.5, -0.5, 0.0,  // Vertex 2\n     0.0,  0.5, 0.0   // Vertex 3\n]);\n\n// 3. Create GPU buffer and upload data\nconst vbo = gl.createBuffer();\ngl.bindBuffer(gl.ARRAY_BUFFER, vbo);\ngl.bufferData(gl.ARRAY_BUFFER, vertices, gl.STATIC_DRAW);\n\n// 4. Configure Attribute pointers\ngl.vertexAttribPointer(0, 3, gl.FLOAT, false, 0, 0);\ngl.enableVertexAttribArray(0);\n\n// 5. Render loop routine\nfunction drawFrame() {\n    // Clear screen\n    gl.clearColor(0.08, 0.08, 0.1, 1.0);\n    gl.clear(gl.COLOR_BUFFER_BIT | gl.DEPTH_BUFFER_BIT);\n    \n    // Draw using active shader program and bound buffer arrays\n    gl.drawArrays(gl.TRIANGLES, 0, 3);\n    \n    requestAnimationFrame(drawFrame);\n}\n\n// Start rendering\nrequestAnimationFrame(drawFrame);\n```\n",
    "title": "WebGL Browser API",
    "tagline": "WebGL 1.0 vs 2.0 specs, JavaScript-to-GPU typed arrays, and draw call batching.",
    "category": "Game Developer",
